WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.320
OK, we are going to continue
our scaling journey.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.645
Today's a bit of a
grab bag of stuff.

00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:13.520
I mean, we're really
going to talk about some

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.480
of the more advanced
details of scaling laws

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.680
and how we scale up models.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.480
And in doing so,
we're going to cover

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.960
what I feel like are some
important details in how

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.280
you would scale up a
language model in practice.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.640
So we might want to ask some
questions, like the scaling law

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.320
stuff that you talked
about two lectures ago,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.000
does that actually work at
real building open-source model

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.600
scales?

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.980
It would be nice if we
could optimize some of this.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.240
There's a lot of learning
rate tricks and other things

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.800
that people do that I
think you should know

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.600
as people that are
informed about how

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.260
to build language models.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.400
So we'll talk about some of
these optimization details.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.840
And then I think the last
thing I want to talk about

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.980
is, when we scale up, there
are certain things that

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.740
are going to be a little
bit trickier to tune.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.580
We know that optimizers
and optimizer behavior

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.060
is going to be actually
pretty scale sensitive.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.520
And because of that, things
like initializations,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.260
learning rates,
batch size, those

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.200
we need to carefully think
about as a function of scale.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.100
So we will talk about how to
think about those carefully.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.140
So up until now, most
of our discussions

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.300
is what I would call the
classical scaling laws cannon.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.680
We talked about Kaplan.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.300
We talked about, oh, even
Hestnes and then the Chinchilla

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:32.840
paper.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.780
So we're up to date with, I
don't know, 2022 or something.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.860
Since then, there
have been a number

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.620
of scaling papers
that are published

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.028
by people who train big models.

00:01:44.028 --> 00:01:45.820
Although, I think the
number of such papers

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.020
have decreased in recent
years, and most of them

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:51.340
have come out of the
open-source community in China.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.660
And so today, I want to
talk about a number of these

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.670
that I've curated and selected
to give you a sense of,

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.950
what does scaling at
the frontier, at least

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.648
open frontier, look like?

00:02:02.648 --> 00:02:04.190
What are the things
that these people

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.570
care about or worry about?

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.430
And what would you
need to do to speedrun

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.990
your way from Chinchilla
all the way to, let's say,

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:15.790
Kimi K2, the most recent release
that has some scaling details.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.090
So that's going to be the
first part of this lecture.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.310
We're going to go through
a couple of papers,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.150
and we're going to go
through the details.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.310
The second part is going to
be a discussion of optimizers

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.190
and initializations.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.070
And remember, I spent a
nontrivial amount of time

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.585
two lectures ago on the
selection of batch sizes.

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:34.210
That's going to be
an important factor.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:35.990
And it's also going
to be important

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.430
thinking about how we're
going to tune things

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.370
like learning rates.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.690
Those are very sensitive,
very important to get right.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.670
And so it turns out
that a lot of effort

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:45.850
has been invested into
getting those things right.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.348
And we will talk about some
of the techniques to do so.

00:02:48.348 --> 00:02:50.390
And this may, in fact,
even be useful in your day

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.845
to day lives of training
various language models

00:02:52.845 --> 00:02:54.470
because they'll give
you a sense of how

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.595
to change your hyperparameters
and your experiments

00:02:56.595 --> 00:02:58.570
as you scale up or down.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:02.690
So I'm going to spend a
nontrivial amount of time

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.490
talking about two
papers in particular.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.210
They're not the newest papers.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.290
They're not necessarily
the most-- actually,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.970
DeepSeek one might be one
of the more detailed ones--

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.950
most detailed scaling
paper in general.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.950
But I think this
hits a sweet spot.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.530
So both of these papers are
serious scaling investigations

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.160
from people who build reasonably
high-performance models.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.410
And importantly, they take
pretty different approaches

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.995
to some of the key questions
that we will ask today.

00:03:29.995 --> 00:03:31.370
And so I think
they're a good way

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:33.870
to motivate a lot of
the rest of the lecture.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.510
So I'm going to talk
about these two.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.730
First one is MiniCPM.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.530
This was, I think, back in 2024,
high-performance small language

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:42.267
model.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:44.350
And DeepSeek-- you all are
now familiar with this.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.142
I no longer have to
justify why I'm talking

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:48.370
about DeepSeek, which is great.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.730
We'll start by talking
about MiniCPM, though.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.630
I do like this paper, especially
when it first came out.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:57.830
It felt very fresh and new
and at the cutting edge.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.910
Even in 2026, I feel
a lot of the things

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:03.710
that the CPM folks did has
been informative to us.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.610
I'll talk about this later
as I get to the newer papers.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.150
But I think it's
becoming the case

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.990
that a lot of this
classic scaling

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.150
stuff, how you set learning
rates, How do you do Chinchilla?

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.649
those kinds of things I think
are now taken for granted.

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:19.105
Everyone just assumes that
you how to do those things.

00:04:19.105 --> 00:04:21.230
And so people don't really
talk about those as much

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:24.350
anymore in the paper, so it's
really nice to talk about some

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.440
of these older papers
for that reason.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.110
So MiniCPM is an
effort by a bunch

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:34.770
of industry academic
partnerships folks over

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.270
in the Chinese
open-source community

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.630
to come up with a
high-performance, small-ish

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:40.410
language model.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.230
And at the time
when this came out,

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:44.880
it was basically state
of the art for the 1 to 2

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.470
billion parameter bracket.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.730
Nowadays, Gemma
and other models--

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.950
Jemma-- and other models at 2D
or 3D are going to be higher

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:52.530
performance.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.340
But these are competitive,
well-trained models,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.520
at least as at 2024 standards.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:04:59.900
So how do you
train these models?

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:05.720
As I said before, we have to get
some of these scaling parameters

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:06.220
right.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.800
And one of the things that
I'll talk about today that I'll

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.520
emphasize quite a bit
is, initializations

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.840
are very important.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.840
Initializations are
important because they

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.160
allow you to make certain
hyperparameters less scale

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.583
dependent.

00:05:20.583 --> 00:05:22.000
So the first
technique that I want

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.160
to talk about from
this MiniCPM paper

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.680
is a special class of
initializations called muP.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.860
So muP, the whole point of
this approach is to say,

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.480
I want to make sure
that my optimal learning

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.000
rate is the same as
I scale up and down.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.735
I talked about this very
briefly in the two lectures ago,

00:05:41.735 --> 00:05:43.860
so you should hopefully
remember that a little bit.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.040
But this is the first
real instantiation

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.880
that we're going to talk about.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.680
What does that look
like specifically?

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.540
The MiniCPM paper has a
nice list of things to do,

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.960
so they scale the
embedding output.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.500
They scale the
residual connections

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.860
by the square root of
the number of layers.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.700
They scale the
initialization of all

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:05.940
of their matrix-shaped tensors
by the fan-in, fan-out ratio.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:07.753
They scale the learning
rates of tensors.

00:06:07.753 --> 00:06:09.420
And you should pay
attention to this one

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.340
because this is
exotic if you aren't

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.060
used to per parameter
learning rates.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.132
And then they also
scale the LM heads.

00:06:16.132 --> 00:06:17.840
We'll talk about why
you do these things,

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.900
or how you derive this
particular set of scalings.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.228
Suffice to say that you're
just going to change up

00:06:23.228 --> 00:06:25.020
how you're going to do
your initializations

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.520
and your learning
rates a little bit

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.020
in the hopes of stabilizing your
learning rates a little bit.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:34.080
And of course, we're
training a small model here,

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.360
but we're still wanting to
do a nice scaling strategy.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.180
Part of the point
of this study is not

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.880
that you want to brute
force a 1.5 model,

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.218
like training hundreds of them.

00:06:43.218 --> 00:06:45.260
You want to get the
hyperparameters exactly right

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:45.885
the first time.

00:06:45.885 --> 00:06:49.380
So you're going to train a bunch
much smaller models and then get

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:54.240
a scaling ladder up all the way
to your target size of 5x or so.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.618
And so there's about a 5x gap
between the biggest model they

00:06:57.618 --> 00:07:00.160
train on their ladder and the
actual model that they're going

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:01.300
to finally release.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.420
And their goal is
going to nail down,

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:05.920
I think, what most
people consider

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.620
to be the sensitive parameters,
like optimal batch size,

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:09.672
learning rate.

00:07:09.672 --> 00:07:11.880
And then the token-to-size
ratio that we talked about

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.040
at Chinchilla.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:15.700
They also want to replicate
a Chinchilla analysis,

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.800
much less important to us
now and probably to you

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:19.825
even if you're training.

00:07:19.825 --> 00:07:22.200
But I'm going to talk about
it regardless because there's

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:23.760
one important thing
in the way they

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.920
do that and that I think has
been informative and useful.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.960
So as I said earlier,
the whole point

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.080
of going through this
weird initialization

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.840
or nonstandard
initialization called

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:38.800
muP is to try to make the
optimal learning rate stable.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:39.720
Is it?

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.920
At least in the MiniCPM
case, the answer is yes.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.420
In fact, it's very, very
clean in their experiments.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.140
They sweep learning rate
across a variety of models,

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:50.090
so each line is a model size.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:54.170
The minimal loss is basically
right on 10 to the negative 2.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.990
I guess the minima slightly
shifted for the smallest model.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.890
But even then, it's quite
close to the minimum.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:03.270
So this is a nice success
case of the muP approach.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.930
We will talk about
several other models

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:07.290
and other papers that use muP.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.710
This is not the
only way to do it,

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:10.710
but this is one
effective way to do it.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.330
And if you can get
something like this

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:16.210
now you've removed the need
to tune the learning rate.

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:19.450
But of course, even if you have
a nice parameterization that

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.310
fixes your learning rate
to a single of optimum,

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:26.190
your optimal batch size is
still not going to be the same.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:28.730
So the batch size
actually varies

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:34.010
as a function of both data
set size and model size.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:37.470
So in their case, they run
these model training runs.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.570
So each column of points
that you see on these plots

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:42.270
is a training run.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.168
So they have a fixed
batch size, and they

00:08:44.168 --> 00:08:46.210
increase the number of
tokens processed, I guess,

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.870
from bottom to top.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:49.310
And then they ask,
OK, we've done

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:50.610
many of these training runs.

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:54.510
What is the optimal batch size
fitting these quadratic curves

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:59.990
onto each of these lines over
here, this equal loss contour?

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.070
What they find is this
kind of optimal batch size

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.490
scaling as a function of the
number of tokens processed,

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:08.990
and they do this across
different model sizes.

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:13.230
Maybe you would not be surprised
by this, given the nice scalings

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:16.570
that we saw in the Kaplan
paper for critical batch sizes.

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:20.030
But the optimal batch size
does have a power law structure

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.830
with respect to the target loss.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.950
And so we see exactly
the Kaplan-style analysis

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:28.610
of, if your loss is
lower on the left,

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:30.070
your batch size
should be bigger,

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.950
critical batch size scaling.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.350
And so now given
a particular loss

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:37.830
target, which you can back
out from the scaling laws,

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:41.023
you can basically set what the
optimal or critical batch sizes

00:09:41.023 --> 00:09:42.690
that would give you
the best trade-offs.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.570
So there's a nice
clean trend here.

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:48.370
This will let you set batch
size very easily and precisely.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:52.232
So those two, the batch size
and the learning rate scaling,

00:09:52.232 --> 00:09:54.190
they're going to come
back over and over again.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:56.650
Those are arguably the most
important and sensitive

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:59.650
parameters to do
scaling laws on.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:03.130
But I'll talk about one last
thing in the MiniCPM paper

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.290
that I think is important.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:08.250
So remember, to
do Chinchilla, we

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:10.070
need to train a lot
of different models.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.990
So we're going to,
let's say, do IsoFLOPs.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.150
That means I'm going
to fix a FLOPs budget,

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:16.530
and I'm going to vary my
trade-offs between number

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:18.952
of tokens and model size.

00:10:18.952 --> 00:10:20.410
And to do that,
I'm going to, let's

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.243
say, increase the amount
of data I train on,

00:10:22.243 --> 00:10:24.310
so increase the
number of sequences.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:26.070
But as I increase the
number of sequences,

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.653
remember that each one of these
gets a different learning rate

00:10:28.653 --> 00:10:29.490
schedule.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:31.550
You all training on
cosine learning rates.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.270
Cosine learning
rates look different.

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:35.290
You need to know
the terminal total

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:37.870
budget before you train in
order to have a cosine schedule.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:41.590
And so this leads
to an annoying fact,

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:44.210
which is that you just need to
keep retraining your models.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:47.510
If you want to train a 8
million sequence model,

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:51.390
you can't restart from the end
of a 4-million sequence model.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:52.950
You have to just
restart from scratch

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:54.742
because your learning
rate schedule is just

00:10:54.742 --> 00:10:55.750
fundamentally different.

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:56.930
This is just very annoying.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:00.110
It's like a quadratic cost
in some sense of doing this

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:02.750
because you have to keep
restarting your runs just

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:04.670
from the start.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:07.870
And this seems very wasteful,
and I think lots of other people

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:09.190
did, too.

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:13.870
And I think one of the nice
solutions from the MiniCPM paper

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:16.787
is what's now called, I
think, the warmup stable decay

00:11:16.787 --> 00:11:17.370
learning rate.

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.910
Or this is the
popular term for this,

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:22.230
and it's basically
a big trapezoid.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.930
So you've got a warmup phase.

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:25.430
The warmup phase
is usually defined

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.750
as a constant number of
steps, not as a fraction

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.250
of your training target.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:32.390
So this is independent
of your training horizon.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:34.870
You've got a fairly long
run where you're holding

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.090
the learning rate constant.

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:37.815
That's the stable phase.

00:11:37.815 --> 00:11:40.190
And then at the end, you're
going to decay fairly rapidly

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:40.970
down to 0.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:42.450
That's the decay phase.

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:46.680
And generally, the decay phase
is something like 10% to 20%

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.080
of your total training run.

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:50.400
So you're going to be
stable for the vast majority

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:53.040
of your training run and then
do a very rapid decay down

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.080
to usually about
10% of your maximum

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.820
learning rate at
the very end here.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:02.000
And that's a very
typical warmup, stable,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.960
decay learning rate.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:04.980
And why do we do this?

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.272
What's the point of doing this?

00:12:06.272 --> 00:12:08.840
Well, we do WSD
because now we can

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:12.220
restart a run at any point at
the end of the stable phase.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:15.160
So if I want to reuse a run
and train it for longer,

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:18.787
I'll just roll back my training
to the last stable checkpoint.

00:12:18.787 --> 00:12:21.120
And then I'll just run the
stable learning rates forward

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.340
and then decay the
model down to 0.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:25.920
So this provides a
very easy way for us

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:29.520
to essentially run scale
in data experiments

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.380
without constantly
rerunning your experiments.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:35.580
So you do have to
redecay every time,

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:37.680
but that's maybe 10%
of the total cost.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.720
It's a much better situation
to be repeatedly decaying

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.440
than to repeatedly rerun
your pre-training runs.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:48.580
And this is a very common trick
that's used in many places.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:52.020
I'll show, I guess, a
performance comparison.

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:54.140
There have been many
plots of this form.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:58.900
I think, who was the most
recent folks that were--

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.260
maybe it was Martin
Jaggi and others,

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.820
I think, over at ETH
had an extensive study

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:06.160
of WSD performance.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.580
But I think a lot of papers
have shown similar things of,

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.520
you have a well-tuned
cosine learning curve.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.680
This is the orange
line over here.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.680
And then you've got
various WSD curves.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:18.060
And WSD curves always
look a little bit funky

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.500
because it looks like you're
way underperforming until you

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.140
hit the decay phase,
where suddenly you

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.360
reclaim all of your gains.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:27.020
And then you match or
even sometimes exceed

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:29.500
the cosine learning
rate performance.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:32.205
Anecdotally, I think if you talk
to a bunch of people training

00:13:32.205 --> 00:13:33.580
networks, I think
a lot of people

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.500
will say the cosine is
slightly better in many cases.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:40.040
But WSD is basically as good
in many other situations.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:42.760
It is a very versatile
default learning rate.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:45.000
It doesn't necessarily
suffer from having

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:49.560
to rerun if you want
to just continue runs.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.400
It is very cool though, to
see just how much of an impact

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.540
learning rate decay has if you
haven't played with that before.

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.760
This part of decaying
the learning rate

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:02.320
is just incredibly important
to getting the final annealing

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:06.000
parts of your training together.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:09.440
OK, so that's the
comparison there.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:13.040
So now that we're equipped with
WSD SGD style learning rates,

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:15.385
Chinchilla style analyzes
are actually very easy.

00:14:15.385 --> 00:14:16.760
What you're going
to do is you're

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:19.020
going to essentially
run one long run,

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:22.200
and then you'll just rewind the
checkpoints and then repeatedly

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:22.700
decay.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:25.540
And that will give you sweeps
along the data dimension.

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.060
And then you'll also do sweeps
along the model dimension.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:30.360
You can do flops or any
other kind of analysis

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:31.640
that you would like.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:33.700
The MiniCPM authors,
for whatever reason,

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.440
choose to try to
replicate methods

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:38.930
1 and 3, which, to me,
are the least reliable

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:40.630
of the Chinchilla methods.

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:44.090
But they do get fairly nice
looking scaling curves.

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:48.150
If you look at the MiniCPM
Chinchilla method 1,

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:52.010
they see nice, lower envelopes
that look mostly linear

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.770
across these, which is good.

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:58.250
And then method 3, they
get nice, smooth scaling

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.130
for the most part
in both the compute

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:03.110
to nonembedding
parameter trade-off.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.870
So it seems like,
for the most part,

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:07.710
they get reasonable
Chinchilla-looking fits.

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:10.570
One thing I will
point out is that they

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:14.970
get pretty different exponents
for their overall joint fit

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:17.010
compared to Chinchilla.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.210
They claim in the
paper that this

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:21.530
means that they should
be training with way more

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:23.550
pieces of text than Chinchilla.

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:26.608
It is highly unclear to me
whether this is a really true

00:15:26.608 --> 00:15:28.650
or whether their Chinchilla
fits are a little bit

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:33.130
strange relative to
the Chinchilla paper.

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:35.290
OK, I'll stop here for
a moment in case someone

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:36.990
has questions about MiniCPM.

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:38.990
Maybe the main couple
things to take away

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:42.690
is, one is the use of
muP is a useful trick,

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.350
I guess, to try to stabilize
how your learning rate changes

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:46.398
over scales.

00:15:46.398 --> 00:15:48.190
And then the second
thing maybe to remember

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:49.317
is the WSD learning rate.

00:15:49.317 --> 00:15:50.650
That's just a very common trick.

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:52.730
You should all know
about this learning rate.

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:55.110
That's a basic thing to know.

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:57.690
OK, good.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:01.590
All right, so then I'll
move on to DeepSeek.

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.550
This is the original
DeepSeek paper,

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:07.150
before they started
talking about MOEs and all

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:09.350
these other very cool things.

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:11.650
And I think, even at the
very first DeepSeek paper,

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:14.230
you could tell that these
were very serious people

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:16.830
because I think DeepSeek
LLM, even now, is,

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:20.550
I think, one of the more nicely
executed scaling analyses

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:21.530
in the open world.

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:23.915
And I feel like they
have lots of great taste

00:16:23.915 --> 00:16:25.290
in the experiments
that they run.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:28.370
So even before R1
and so on came out,

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:31.890
it was kind of clear that
these people were going places.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:37.410
All right, so DeepSeek takes a
very different scaling strategy

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:42.250
than MiniCPM and several
others in trying to optimize

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:43.330
their hyperparameters.

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:44.990
They're not going
to really do muP.

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:48.690
They're not going to do this
interesting scaling adjustments.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:53.050
Instead, they're going to
try to fit a scaling law

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:55.050
to estimate both the
optimal batch size

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:56.590
and the optimal learning rate.

00:16:56.590 --> 00:17:00.050
So we that the learning
rate and batch sizes

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:01.912
should actually
change with scale.

00:17:01.912 --> 00:17:03.370
But if the way in
which they change

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:05.329
are predictable, just
like a scaling law,

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:06.290
then we're good to go.

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:09.970
We don't really need to
worry about very much.

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:12.890
So what they do is they
basically run extensive grid

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:15.175
searches at various
different scales,

00:17:15.175 --> 00:17:16.550
and they try to
find the optimum.

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:19.377
So this is an example of the
kind of thing they're doing.

00:17:19.377 --> 00:17:20.710
They're sweeping learning rates.

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:22.109
They're sweeping batch sizes.

00:17:22.109 --> 00:17:24.770
And they're looking
at the terminal loss

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:27.890
if they're processing a
particular number of tokens.

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:31.570
So they fixed the compute,
and then they vary these two.

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:34.230
They'll do this across
several different scales,

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:35.980
and then they'll
basically try to identify

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.700
what the lowest loss is, so in
this case, the lightest color.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.740
So they'll put a
little star there,

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.480
and then they'll do
this across scales.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.700
So if you do this across
sufficiently many scales,

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:48.800
you will get a plot that
looks a little like this.

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:51.860
And this plot is
saying, OK, I'm going

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:56.240
to vary my nonembedding trading
flops across some range.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.120
And you can see these
gray dots over here.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:03.980
And as I vary this, my optimal
batch size will change.

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:06.100
Remember, the
optimal batch size is

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:11.980
derived by looking at these big
grids and finding the minimizer.

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:14.840
And I will get something
that looks like a line.

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:16.580
The optimal batch
size should scale

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:18.540
with my nonembedding FLOPs.

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:21.060
And similarly for my
optimal learning rate,

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:26.260
I'll get a bunch of gray dots
in my nonembedding FLOPs range.

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:27.900
And then I can
fit a line to that

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:29.820
to try to estimate what
my optimal learning

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:30.960
rate should look like.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:35.980
So this is fairly nice.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:37.480
And I guess one
thing I'll point out

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:40.820
is, their full runs
are on the star.

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:43.080
These are the runs
that they chose.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:46.320
At higher FLOPs, they have
much higher batch sizes

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:47.660
and much lower learning rates.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:52.000
The stars are the things they
actually ran for their big runs.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:55.920
These fits look reasonable
for the batches.

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:57.920
This does look like a line.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:18:59.960
The one on the learning rate--

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.760
I'm not quite sure
that constitutes

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:05.920
the best linear fits that
I've seen in my life,

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:07.140
but I guess it works.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:08.480
It's probably fine.

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:09.540
The models do train.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:11.158
The models do get
reasonable things.

00:19:11.158 --> 00:19:12.700
I think this is the
one, I would say,

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:14.575
like drawback of the
learning rates approach.

00:19:14.575 --> 00:19:17.080
It's not really clear that if
you try to do this grid search

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:19.820
kind of thing, unless your
grid is in the right place,

00:19:19.820 --> 00:19:21.680
you've got a lot of
quantization error.

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:25.183
And then you're not getting
very good scaling law fits,

00:19:25.183 --> 00:19:27.600
and you end up with something
that looks like an arbitrary

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:30.160
linear thing going on.

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:33.940
OK, oh, yeah, sorry.

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:37.840
How come for the same number
of training swaps there,

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.700
you get multiple data
points with a large range

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:43.180
of optimal vertices?

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:46.100
Why is it that you get a large
range of optimal learning rates

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:47.360
for different training FLOPs?

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:49.660
Is that the question on
the y-axis over here?

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:53.060
Yeah, so the reason
I think there

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:55.980
is because of the changes
to the model sizes

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:57.000
and things like this.

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:05.860
So 2024 was back
in the era where

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:09.300
I think every open model
builder was replicating

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.500
the entire scaling law stack.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:18.020
And so DeepSeek also complements
the learning rate analysis

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:21.660
with basically a
Chinchilla replication.

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:23.580
And to do that, they're
also going to do

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:24.840
the same thing as MiniCPM.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:27.500
They're going to follow the
WSD-style learning rate.

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:29.060
They have a weird
little variation

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:32.150
where they do two decay
phases instead of one.

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:35.510
I am not really
sure why this is.

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:38.010
It hasn't really been a thing
that has taken off afterwards,

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:39.210
but it is a thing that they do.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:41.030
Regardless, the whole
point here is to say,

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:43.310
WSD is an effective approach.

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:46.310
And you can use that to
replicate Chinchilla.

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:50.470
And DeepSeek has much
nicer scaling curves

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:53.410
than MiniCPM because of
their choice to go IsoFLOPs.

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:56.830
So they get these very
nice, clean sweeps

00:20:56.830 --> 00:20:59.050
across the fixed flops ranges.

00:20:59.050 --> 00:21:01.910
And then they also get
fairly nice and clean

00:21:01.910 --> 00:21:05.950
Chinchilla-style
trade-offs as before.

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:07.830
So this allows them to
essentially replicate

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:12.670
the Chinchilla results and get
a similar kind of data model

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:13.717
scaling law.

00:21:13.717 --> 00:21:15.550
Really, the point that
I'm showing with this

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.830
is just to say, look,
the Chinchilla laws have

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:21.910
been replicated by other people
at large-ish compute ranges.

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:25.270
So that analysis
is generally sound.

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:28.053
Finally, I think, this is the--

00:21:28.053 --> 00:21:29.970
I'm a little bit sad
that these kinds of plots

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:33.390
are now somewhat rare in
open-source release papers,

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:35.450
but this is really
the final punchline

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:37.050
of a lot of scaling law work.

00:21:37.050 --> 00:21:40.210
You train a bunch of
small, curated models

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:44.630
at smaller compute ranges, and
then you draw out the power law.

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:46.370
And then in the end,
you get something

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:49.190
pretty close to reality
if you scale out enough.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:51.670
So the two stars are the models
that they actually trained.

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:54.250
The gray dots are
their scaling law fits.

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:57.310
And they're getting pretty
good predictions on their star.

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:59.650
I mean, it could be better,
but it's not bad at all.

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:02.970
And so they're able to get some
pretty accurate scaling law

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:08.330
predictions on real open-source
models that they've trained.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:10.370
OK, I'm actually I'll stop here.

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:12.850
If people have questions
about the DeepSeek stuff,

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:13.950
feel free to ask them.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:16.890
I think my one-line
summary here is to say,

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:18.732
this is the other
approach to dealing

00:22:18.732 --> 00:22:20.190
with the sensitive
hyperparameters.

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.050
You can either attempt
to stabilize them,

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:23.990
or you can attempt
to just scaling law

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:26.270
fit them and then just
go with the scaling law.

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:29.750
And DeepSeek represents the more
fit the scaling law approach

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:32.550
to this problem.

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:34.870
Good, OK.

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:39.110
So there are other
models, more recent ones

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:42.910
that have nice scaling
laws that they talk about.

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:45.710
As I said before, though,
I think a lot of this work

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:46.630
is now--

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:50.730
what's the-- it's all
known to everybody.

00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:53.070
And so no one is really
putting a lot of this stuff

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.890
into big sections
into the paper.

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:57.530
So if you look at Qwen 2.5,
they say something like,

00:22:57.530 --> 00:23:00.230
OK, well, we need to tune
some optimal hyperparameters,

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:02.245
like batch sizes
and learning rates.

00:23:02.245 --> 00:23:03.870
So we do a bunch of
scaling experiments

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.392
to figure out what
the optimum are.

00:23:05.392 --> 00:23:07.850
And then we have a scaling law,
and we predict the optimum.

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:09.970
And then we're able to
do that even for MOEs.

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:14.350
So this is basically the same
thing as the DeepSeek analysis

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:16.790
that they're talking
about in Qwen 2.5.

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:17.890
Same thing with Qwen 3.

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:20.230
By the time it's
Qwen 3, they say,

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:22.730
all right, we've already figured
out what to do in Qwen 2.5.

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:24.397
We're just doing
exactly the same thing.

00:23:24.397 --> 00:23:27.240
So this has become part
of a very standard recipe

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:30.520
that you use in order to nail
down the optimal learning

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:31.580
rates and batches.

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:37.760
Kimi K2 is probably
the newest paper

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.688
that I'll talk
about of the series.

00:23:39.688 --> 00:23:41.480
At this point, I'm just
going to quickly go

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:44.000
through several papers
maybe to give you

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.640
an overview of what
people are doing

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:51.360
in terms of scaling work in
the open-source model releases.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:56.400
So Kimi K2 and a few others
have focused on essentially MoE

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:57.440
scaling laws.

00:23:57.440 --> 00:23:59.100
So it's 2026.

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:02.120
Most people know how to do a
Chinchilla scaling law or even

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:03.600
a learning rate scaling law.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:04.580
What things are new?

00:24:04.580 --> 00:24:07.300
Well, a lot of these companies
are now switching to MoEs,

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:08.380
fully switching to MoEs.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:11.280
And if you're doing that,
you really want to know,

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:13.727
am I training the right
amount of active parameters?

00:24:13.727 --> 00:24:15.560
And so they're doing
things, like, OK, we're

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:18.660
going to vary the flops
and look at my losses.

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:21.320
And I'm going to do so for
different levels of sparsity

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:25.060
so that I can figure out how
much sparsity helps or hurts

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:26.042
my losses.

00:24:26.042 --> 00:24:27.500
And their conclusion,
of course, is

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:30.100
if you look at the different
sparsity levels, the more sparse

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:33.640
your network, the better
your validation loss,

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:35.760
given a FLOP-- unsurprising.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:38.060
But now the fact that
they can quantitatively

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:41.300
know what the relationship
is between sparsity and loss

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:43.980
means that they can make
more rational decisions

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:46.853
about how much sparsity
to put into their model.

00:24:46.853 --> 00:24:48.520
And they look at this,
and they say, OK,

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:51.020
what we're going to do is we're
going to use sparsity of 48,

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:53.312
because at that point we're
hitting diminishing returns

00:24:53.312 --> 00:24:54.400
on our scaling loss.

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:57.900
So you can see how
this kind of analysis

00:24:57.900 --> 00:25:01.540
is used to justify different
architectural decisions

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:04.280
across different
open-source releases.

00:25:04.280 --> 00:25:08.740
Hunyuan, which is another
open-source MoE from a few years

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:11.860
ago now, they do a
very similar thing.

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:14.500
I think every company, once
they switch to the MoE,

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:17.380
has to do this kind
of MoE scaling law

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:19.620
to figure out how the
number of activated

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:21.920
parameters or the
amount of sparsity city

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:22.920
changes training loss.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:25.740
And then they redo a
lot of these analyses.

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:27.560
So for example,
in Hunyuan's case,

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:32.138
they end up with a 96 data point
per active parameter ratio.

00:25:32.138 --> 00:25:34.180
In their case, they're
fixing the sparsity level.

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:37.880
And so that's not a thing
that they're searching over.

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:40.840
LLaMA 3 also has some
nice scaling laws.

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:44.600
Unfortunately, they're not
particularly useful ones.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:46.880
They do IsoFLOP-style
scaling and get

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:50.880
slightly different but similar
ratios for token-to-model.

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:53.920
The most interesting thing that
they do in terms of scaling

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:56.920
in LLaMA 3 is the
plot on the right

00:25:56.920 --> 00:26:00.000
here, which is, they
have the usual scaling

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.340
law, which is the left panel.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:03.860
That's saying, if I
have more compute,

00:26:03.860 --> 00:26:08.400
then the logprob on the
answer for a benchmark

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.300
decays consistently.

00:26:10.300 --> 00:26:13.080
And on the right side,
they show, OK, well,

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:15.960
if you have better
log loss, then

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:18.163
the downstream
accuracy also goes up.

00:26:18.163 --> 00:26:19.580
So they're basically
saying, well,

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:22.150
we can just fit a sigmoid
on these purple points.

00:26:22.150 --> 00:26:24.410
And that might give
us a sense of how good

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:26.010
these different models are.

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.670
You can see their systematic
deviations from the curve.

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:30.490
So I don't really
know if I would really

00:26:30.490 --> 00:26:33.230
buy that this curve is like
the one truth that's going on.

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:34.605
But you can
certainly see that it

00:26:34.605 --> 00:26:37.530
is consistent with
a sigmoidal scaling

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:39.730
mapping from the loss to
the accuracy, which I think

00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:41.970
is very useful because
we've been talking only

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.790
about log losses.

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:47.210
But there is a tight coupling
in some cases, or in many cases,

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:50.550
between log losses and
downstream accuracy.

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:59.130
And then for one
last paper that I

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:03.510
want to talk about for scaling,
MiniMax-01 from last year

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:05.335
had this nice set of
scaling experiments

00:27:05.335 --> 00:27:07.210
where they were doing
the exact kind of thing

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:10.770
I was saying is a useful
affordance of scaling

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:13.990
from two lectures ago, and that
is architecture variations.

00:27:13.990 --> 00:27:15.550
And so they wanted
to understand,

00:27:15.550 --> 00:27:17.250
OK, I have lightning
attention, which

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:20.330
is some kind of a linear
attention architecture.

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:22.610
I have a softmax version
like full attention,

00:27:22.610 --> 00:27:24.890
and I have a hybrid
version that mixes the two.

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:26.553
How do these things scale?

00:27:26.553 --> 00:27:28.470
Is it the case that if
I scale up, one of them

00:27:28.470 --> 00:27:30.550
will get much worse
than the other?

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:34.750
And so you can see, they're
replicating essentially

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:38.630
the Kaplan plots of,
as I increase FLOPs,

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.550
how do the learning
curves change?

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:42.990
And is the number
of parameters that I

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:45.350
need different for
different levels of compute

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:46.970
for these different
architectures?

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.630
And the answer is,
for the most part, no,

00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:52.070
that hybrid and
lightning and softmax

00:27:52.070 --> 00:27:53.850
are all comparable
for the most part.

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:56.290
And they require
similar model sizes.

00:27:56.290 --> 00:27:58.990
And this set of plots
is used to justify

00:27:58.990 --> 00:28:00.710
their use of the
hybrid architecture

00:28:00.710 --> 00:28:02.110
in the actual deployed system.

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.830
So this is exactly the kind of
decision making that you would

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:09.330
do based upon a scaling law.

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:11.390
So that's basically
what I wanted

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:14.710
to cover for the recent
scaling law recipes

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:16.430
from the various companies.

00:28:16.430 --> 00:28:20.050
So the two things, as I said
before, for the DeepSeek recipe.

00:28:20.050 --> 00:28:21.978
Really, the key thing
is the scaling analysis

00:28:21.978 --> 00:28:23.270
on the batch and learning rate.

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:25.250
This is the
scaling-based approach.

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:28.090
And MiniCPM follows a more
of a muP-style approach

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:30.810
to try to get invariances.

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:32.590
And then we see some trends.

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:35.810
We see that Kimi and Qwen
and others, or in Hunyuan,

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:38.370
are doing these MoE
scaling experiments

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:39.730
as they switch to MoEs.

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:42.610
We see some architecture
work by MiniMax,

00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:45.170
and we also see some
IsoFLOPs analysis being

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:47.890
replicated by several others.

00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:49.735
And to return to
our previous point,

00:28:49.735 --> 00:28:51.610
the point here is, I
think a lot of companies

00:28:51.610 --> 00:28:54.090
are now less detailed
because the core scaling

00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:57.630
machinery, like doing Chinchilla
or doing learning rate scaling,

00:28:57.630 --> 00:28:59.750
is now fairly well
understood by everybody.

00:28:59.750 --> 00:29:03.890
So there's no
necessarily extra value

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:06.992
in showing those experiments
for some of these releases.

00:29:06.992 --> 00:29:08.450
Or at least that's
my guess for why

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:10.730
we have much fewer of
these kinds of experiments

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:12.490
in the release.

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:15.730
OK, I'll take one
minute and then pause

00:29:15.730 --> 00:29:18.390
and let you digest and ask a
question or two, in case you

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:19.610
have questions for this part.

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:31.650
OK, so all these cover
the proofing stage.

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:34.310
So my question is,
what changes and what

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:38.510
do not change when no post
training is standardized process

00:29:38.510 --> 00:29:40.790
for model development?

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:43.563
Yeah, so the question
was about, what

00:29:43.563 --> 00:29:45.230
changes about this
when you do-- or when

00:29:45.230 --> 00:29:47.990
you think about post
training, I guess, broadly?

00:29:47.990 --> 00:29:50.910
I think that's still
a big, open question.

00:29:50.910 --> 00:29:54.430
There's no great way to do
fully integrated scaling

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:56.370
with post-training
accounted for,

00:29:56.370 --> 00:29:58.950
because post-training can
sometimes really change

00:29:58.950 --> 00:30:02.630
the nature of what
pre-training you should do.

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:04.390
The closest things
that I can think

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:07.270
of to thinking
about post-training

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:10.750
is there's been some work
on understanding coverage

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:14.600
or diversity notions in
pre-training that would help

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:16.220
predict what happens
in post-training,

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:17.340
but it's still
pretty nascent work.

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:19.880
I don't think there's any good
answer to the question of,

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:25.600
what do we do about
synergy with post-training?

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:26.520
Cool.

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:31.840
OK, so now we're going to
talk about the other piece.

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:35.400
So I think the two remaining
parts of this lecture

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.100
map on cleanly to
the two examples.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:41.700
So I'm going to talk about one
approach, one set of approaches,

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:44.020
which is about scaling
laws for learning rates,

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:45.580
batch sizes, and optimizers.

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:50.080
That corresponds to the
DeepSeek approach to the world.

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:52.860
And then I'm going to
talk about the muP stuff.

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:55.820
That's the MiniCPM
approach to the world.

00:30:55.820 --> 00:30:58.160
Can we just
reparameterize everything

00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:00.860
to make it so that we don't
have to worry about scale?

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:04.400
So we're going to now talk
about the DeepSeek approach.

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:07.240
How do we scale
learning rate properly?

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:09.480
And so there's going to
be a hyperparameter part

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:11.485
and an optimizer choice part.

00:31:11.485 --> 00:31:12.860
And I'm going to
draw quite a bit

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:17.700
from this pretty recent
preprint from the StepFun folks.

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:21.420
I don't think there's
a great, really, truly

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:26.160
reliable, robust study
on hyperparameters,

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:28.300
especially learning rates.

00:31:28.300 --> 00:31:30.560
But this is probably
getting somewhat close.

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:35.400
The StepFun folks train credible
large models and release them.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:37.860
And they burned a ton
of compute in grid

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:40.660
searching the space of
hyperparameters in this scaling

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:41.240
law study.

00:31:41.240 --> 00:31:43.180
So I'll go through
this and show you

00:31:43.180 --> 00:31:45.460
some intriguing
empirical phenomena

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:48.060
about how learning rates,
batch size, and all of those

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:51.300
affect downstream losses.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:56.740
So lots of people have talked
about lots of different ways

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:59.660
of scaling, learning rate
and batch sizes as roughly

00:31:59.660 --> 00:32:01.820
as a function of compute.

00:32:01.820 --> 00:32:04.760
And the StepFun survey--
not survey paper,

00:32:04.760 --> 00:32:07.420
the hyperparameter tuning
paper has this very nice table

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:10.220
that captures a lot of this.

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:12.920
And I'll point out a
couple of different things.

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:14.400
Some of this we already know.

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:17.520
So the very top row, this
OpenAI scaling law-- this

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:18.760
is from Kaplan.

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:20.500
If you look at the
batch size over there,

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:25.700
notice how it says 2eE18,
capital L to the exponent.

00:32:25.700 --> 00:32:27.220
That's critical
batch sizes right.

00:32:27.220 --> 00:32:29.840
It's basically saying, you
should scale your batch size

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:31.740
as a function of
your terminal loss.

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:33.200
Great.

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:34.540
DeepSeek we just saw.

00:32:34.540 --> 00:32:38.840
This is the compute-based
scaling loss for both learning

00:32:38.840 --> 00:32:40.013
rate and batch size.

00:32:40.013 --> 00:32:41.680
And there's been a
couple of other folks

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:44.860
that have proposed various kinds
of scaling, including MiniCPM,

00:32:44.860 --> 00:32:47.840
which we talked about as well.

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:49.840
Another important thing
that I want to point out

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:53.060
is that these formulas for
learning rate and batch sizes,

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:54.760
they don't even have
the same inputs.

00:32:54.760 --> 00:32:56.880
People don't even
seem to agree, what

00:32:56.880 --> 00:32:59.340
are the variables that should
affect these learning rates?

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:01.960
Like, the OpenAI one
is terminal loss.

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:05.000
Whereas, the StepFun
folks, they claim

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:06.720
that the right
batch size scaling

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:08.360
is a function of the data.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:12.010
So there's a lot of different
disagreements about the nature

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:14.810
of scaling for hyperparameters.

00:33:14.810 --> 00:33:17.690
And I think one thing I'll
say as I go through this

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:21.730
is not to take even
this last row as gospel.

00:33:21.730 --> 00:33:24.988
A lot of these things are
pretty brittle in many ways,

00:33:24.988 --> 00:33:26.530
but the experiments
and the phenomena

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:28.197
are things that you
can learn well from.

00:33:28.197 --> 00:33:31.210
So I'll do that.

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:34.930
So the design of this paper is
very similar to the DeepSeek

00:33:34.930 --> 00:33:35.430
one.

00:33:35.430 --> 00:33:38.050
They're just going to
train a ton of models

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:42.170
to grid out both the learning
rate space and the batch size

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:44.810
space and do them across a
range of models and range

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:47.030
of data set sizes.

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.690
And by doing so, they hope
to essentially saturate

00:33:49.690 --> 00:33:52.730
the hyperparameter space
for a set of small models

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:55.470
and then nail down
what the optimum is.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:57.470
And much like in
the DeepSeek design,

00:33:57.470 --> 00:33:59.490
they're going to be--
after they grid up

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:01.990
the space-- in this case,
they have a contour plot.

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:03.490
But the underlying
objects are just,

00:34:03.490 --> 00:34:05.370
they run a bunch of models.

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:08.010
They aim to try to
minimize the losses.

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:09.570
And the contour
plots are quite nice.

00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:13.310
I feel like they give
a good visualization.

00:34:13.310 --> 00:34:16.670
They ran a pretty
high resolution grid.

00:34:16.670 --> 00:34:19.670
And thanks to that, one of
the things that we can see

00:34:19.670 --> 00:34:21.389
is that they can
actually plot out

00:34:21.389 --> 00:34:24.989
a fairly high-resolution contour
of what the hyperparameter

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:25.770
space looks like.

00:34:25.770 --> 00:34:30.610
In this case, this is a slice at
1b parameters and 100b tokens,

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:31.830
I believe.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:33.949
And you can see
on the right side,

00:34:33.949 --> 00:34:37.190
that surface is the
hyperparameter landscape.

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:40.230
As a function of the batch size,
which is one axis, and learning

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:42.469
rate, which is the other
axis, how does the loss

00:34:42.469 --> 00:34:46.710
change if you fix the total
amount of data in the model?

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:49.710
Now, we see that
each slice, if you

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:52.190
fix a batch size or you
fix a learning rate--

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:54.389
each slice is kind
of nice and convex.

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:56.550
And it's almost even smooth.

00:34:56.550 --> 00:34:57.950
And this is quite
nice because it

00:34:57.950 --> 00:35:01.967
means that you can search for
the optimal hyperparameter

00:35:01.967 --> 00:35:04.550
fairly nicely, and you can be
confident that the minima is not

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:05.250
too far off.

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:06.670
This was very jaggedy.

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:08.970
You might have some
very serious doubts

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:11.770
about whether this kind of
program of gridding the space

00:35:11.770 --> 00:35:13.283
is even viable.

00:35:13.283 --> 00:35:15.450
Also, this should maybe
give you some nice intuition

00:35:15.450 --> 00:35:20.290
about the smoothness of the
hyperparameter space in general.

00:35:20.290 --> 00:35:23.050
And I think there's actually
one really interesting

00:35:23.050 --> 00:35:24.730
general scaling trend.

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:28.083
And looking at other related
papers to this topic, I think,

00:35:28.083 --> 00:35:30.250
I don't know if I've seen
any contradicting evidence

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:32.890
to this observation.

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:36.030
So I think the one thing
that seems very interesting

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:37.930
and one of the key
takeaways of this work

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:41.010
is, essentially, if you're
looking at the optimal batch

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:43.770
size, the only thing
that seems to depend on

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:46.650
is the total amount of data
that you're training on.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:49.870
So this right-hand plot
is the one to look at.

00:35:49.870 --> 00:35:52.610
So x-axis here is the amount of
data that you're training on.

00:35:52.610 --> 00:35:55.390
The different colored dots--
those are different models.

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:57.290
Notice how the different
colored dots seem

00:35:57.290 --> 00:35:59.810
to follow roughly the
same trend line here,

00:35:59.810 --> 00:36:01.730
fairly predictable one.

00:36:01.730 --> 00:36:03.530
And this is in
log-log scale, so you

00:36:03.530 --> 00:36:07.780
should expect some of a power
law that's a function of D.

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:11.560
On the left panel, we have the
dependence of learning rate.

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:15.440
And we see that this is a
very different behavior.

00:36:15.440 --> 00:36:18.500
So if you go from blue all the
way to this pink color right,

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:21.860
bigger models have smaller
optimal learning rate.

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:26.880
And larger amounts of data have
higher optimal learning rate,

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:30.620
which is counterintuitive,
actually.

00:36:30.620 --> 00:36:33.440
And so this might
be a little fragile.

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:34.940
There are other
kinds of papers that

00:36:34.940 --> 00:36:37.953
argue that the dependence
on D should be reversed.

00:36:37.953 --> 00:36:40.120
But the trends here are at
least on this experiment,

00:36:40.120 --> 00:36:40.920
are quite clear.

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:48.940
And one final thing
that I want to point out

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:51.960
is, the learning rates are
actually pretty robust.

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:54.460
And I think this is backed
up by a lot of experiences

00:36:54.460 --> 00:36:56.260
and experiments
that people have.

00:36:56.260 --> 00:36:59.700
I think people have a sense
of-- actually as a poll,

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:02.320
do people know what a
good hyperparameter is?

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:04.660
If you were told to train
a language model tomorrow,

00:37:04.660 --> 00:37:07.640
would it be 1e negative
3, 1e negative 4?

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:10.120
I feel like we roughly
have a sense of, actually,

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:13.480
this range of hyperparameters
for learning rates is generally

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:15.440
a good starting point.

00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:17.180
And that is generally true.

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:21.000
So the scaling laws
that they got actually

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:24.720
transfer over to, for example,
MoEs to a certain extent.

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:27.400
The yellow stars, which might
be a little harder to see,

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:29.340
are the predictions
from their scaling laws.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:33.720
The red x's are the optima
that are derived directly

00:37:33.720 --> 00:37:34.780
on the MoEs.

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:37.440
And so you switch to an MoE.

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:39.490
You don't end up with a
very different scaling

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:41.240
law, as long as you're
roughly controlling

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:43.600
for active parameters.

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:46.120
The one thing that does
seem a little bit more

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:48.160
potentially dicey
is, as you shift

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:50.600
the training data,
the optimal learning

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:53.480
rates and the optimal batch
sizes do shift a little bit,

00:37:53.480 --> 00:37:57.020
which means that all of these
phenomena are likely contingent.

00:37:57.020 --> 00:38:00.337
The specific numbers that are
in these scaling laws and so on

00:38:00.337 --> 00:38:01.920
are potentially
contingent to the data

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:04.120
that you train on
more so than others.

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:08.660
And so I want to flash this
back up before I move on.

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:11.660
So you end up with
some of simple scaling

00:38:11.660 --> 00:38:14.540
law at the end, which
says, OK, batch size maybe

00:38:14.540 --> 00:38:17.140
should be roughly square
root of the number of data

00:38:17.140 --> 00:38:18.520
times some constant.

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:22.100
Your learning rate
should scale upwards

00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:24.200
with batch size or
upwards with data

00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:27.120
but downwards with
the model size.

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:31.580
And that's a surprising
effect but not maybe crazy,

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:34.300
because these experiments
often, in many cases,

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:36.300
are assuming Chinchilla-like
scaling, in which

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:38.102
case, those two would cancel.

00:38:38.102 --> 00:38:40.060
And as you increase
compute, your learning rate

00:38:40.060 --> 00:38:41.780
should go down.

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:44.278
And hopefully, that
Chinchilla comment was clear.

00:38:44.278 --> 00:38:46.320
What I mean is when you're
scaling by Chinchilla,

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:49.052
N and D are both
driven by compute.

00:38:49.052 --> 00:38:51.260
And as compute goes up, you
expect your learning rate

00:38:51.260 --> 00:38:51.808
to go down.

00:38:51.808 --> 00:38:54.100
And so you will end up with
something like the DeepSeek

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.500
Law, where the learning
rate decreases with compute,

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:58.420
and the batch size
increases with compute,

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:03.070
albeit with different exponents
than the DeepSeek Law.

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:05.070
Cool.

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:09.990
OK, so in some sense, this
is the newest and most

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:12.930
large-scale version of
the DeepSeek analysis.

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:14.670
And visit one way
that, if you were

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:16.570
doing the
scaling-law-style approach,

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:22.890
you would approach this problem
of fitting a learning rate.

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:25.130
So that's the
hyperparameter story.

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:27.750
If someone has questions,
feel free to stop me.

00:39:27.750 --> 00:39:30.230
And then next, right
after, I'm going to move on

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:32.290
to talking about optimizers.

00:39:32.290 --> 00:39:35.990
I think this is one thing that
didn't have time to talk about

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:38.810
in the architectures lecture.

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:40.910
That was already a
pretty packed lecture.

00:39:40.910 --> 00:39:44.030
But I think it would
be a disservice to if I

00:39:44.030 --> 00:39:45.730
don't talk about optimizers.

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:47.338
And optimizers are
scale dependent,

00:39:47.338 --> 00:39:49.630
so this is a good place to
put them and talk about some

00:39:49.630 --> 00:39:52.630
of the latest and greatest
developments in optimizers

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:55.550
and their relationship
with scale.

00:39:55.550 --> 00:39:57.510
Oh, yeah?

00:39:57.510 --> 00:40:01.110
So I guess I'm trying to figure
out how to interpret this.

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:04.250
Does that mean we should just
take the scaling laws that we

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:08.010
have and then apply them,
rather than running our own grid

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:09.210
search?

00:40:09.210 --> 00:40:11.678
Yeah, I mean, I think it
depends what regime you're in.

00:40:11.678 --> 00:40:13.136
The question is
like-- oh, sorry, I

00:40:13.136 --> 00:40:16.052
have to repeat it for the online
folks-- is should we just take

00:40:16.052 --> 00:40:18.010
the scaling law and run
them, or should we just

00:40:18.010 --> 00:40:20.130
do our own grid search?

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:24.130
It does depend on the amount
of compute budget that you have

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:26.410
and how much you
need to nail it.

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:29.830
I do think, for example,
maybe the StepFun law--

00:40:29.830 --> 00:40:31.430
if you're in the
right compute regime,

00:40:31.430 --> 00:40:33.388
like close to where they
were running the grid,

00:40:33.388 --> 00:40:35.090
these are probably
really good defaults.

00:40:35.090 --> 00:40:38.970
I would bet on these over
something I pull out of my hat.

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:42.570
That said, if you were doing
your own big pre-training run,

00:40:42.570 --> 00:40:44.730
you're probably going to
have minor differences.

00:40:44.730 --> 00:40:46.530
Someone might say,
I really believe

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:48.250
we should have a
big weight decay.

00:40:48.250 --> 00:40:50.290
My architecture has
a big weight decay.

00:40:50.290 --> 00:40:53.290
We don't know necessarily
that those things

00:40:53.290 --> 00:40:55.142
are going to scale the same.

00:40:55.142 --> 00:40:57.350
And so you might want to
actually redo a lot of this.

00:40:57.350 --> 00:41:00.350
And this is why I think a
lot of the reports I showed

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:02.150
you were redoing
Chinchilla, redoing all

00:41:02.150 --> 00:41:05.070
these things, because there's
enough minor differences

00:41:05.070 --> 00:41:06.990
that people want to
check and make sure

00:41:06.990 --> 00:41:11.550
that they're roughly first-order
correct with these results.

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:14.487
I think this will come up again
in this next optimizer part.

00:41:14.487 --> 00:41:16.570
And I was talking about
this with [MUTED] earlier,

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:20.130
which is, scaling laws have this
very scientific feel to them.

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:22.530
It's like, yes, fit these
lines and extrapolate them.

00:41:22.530 --> 00:41:25.990
But ultimately, a big part of
scaling laws is still vibes.

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:28.630
It's like, do I really believe
that the experiment setting

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:31.710
of this StepFun survey
is similar enough to mine

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:32.950
that they will transfer?

00:41:32.950 --> 00:41:34.210
We can't possibly right.

00:41:34.210 --> 00:41:35.930
There are always
tiny differences.

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:43.410
Good, OK, so now I get
to talk about optimizers.

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:45.870
And I think there's been some
really exciting developments

00:41:45.870 --> 00:41:47.670
in LLM optimizers,
and maybe even

00:41:47.670 --> 00:41:49.670
deep learning
optimizers in general

00:41:49.670 --> 00:41:51.790
over the past year or two.

00:41:51.790 --> 00:41:54.670
And so this is an exciting
time to talk about them.

00:41:54.670 --> 00:41:57.880
And so a story that
I want to start with

00:41:57.880 --> 00:42:01.760
and one that should maybe
motivate this subsection

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:04.080
is this comparison over
here, or maybe we'll

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:05.240
start on this left panel.

00:42:05.240 --> 00:42:08.520
So this is from
NanoGPT speedrun, which

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:10.240
inspired our assignment 1.

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:12.640
And there you have a
really teeny, tiny model

00:42:12.640 --> 00:42:15.680
that you're training on a
fairly short amount of time,

00:42:15.680 --> 00:42:18.480
and you want to get the, I
forget, 3 point-some loss

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:21.160
as quickly as possible.

00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:24.340
And many people have been hill
climbing on this benchmark.

00:42:24.340 --> 00:42:27.080
And I think one of the
more remarkable things that

00:42:27.080 --> 00:42:29.920
have happened on the benchmark
is as a benchmark called

00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:34.920
muon in purple over here came
and got really significant gains

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:36.460
over Adam, which is blue.

00:42:36.460 --> 00:42:38.945
It's right underneath the
green line over there.

00:42:38.945 --> 00:42:40.320
So you can just
look at the green

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:41.487
and the purple and that gap.

00:42:41.487 --> 00:42:43.840
And you say, wow,
lots of big gains

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:46.840
from just changing the
optimizer to this muon thing.

00:42:46.840 --> 00:42:49.300
And it's not even slower than
Adam, much slower than Adam.

00:42:49.300 --> 00:42:51.440
That's great.

00:42:51.440 --> 00:42:53.840
But then there's a
question of, well,

00:42:53.840 --> 00:42:56.460
is this thing actually
good at scale?

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:58.340
And then you go and run
some scaling studies,

00:42:58.340 --> 00:43:00.220
and maybe they're
not quite as good.

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:03.140
This is a really tricky
and important part

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:04.540
of how we do research.

00:43:04.540 --> 00:43:06.395
We have a small-scale
experiment.

00:43:06.395 --> 00:43:08.520
We have something that's
scale-dependent, for sure,

00:43:08.520 --> 00:43:10.100
like an optimizer.

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:12.440
And then we see really big
gains in the small scale.

00:43:12.440 --> 00:43:14.860
What do we do about
the large scale?

00:43:14.860 --> 00:43:18.440
And more than providing a
solid, concrete prescription,

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:21.620
I want us to maybe almost
even provide a story here so

00:43:21.620 --> 00:43:24.180
that you can get a
sense of what we can do

00:43:24.180 --> 00:43:27.300
and what things we
can't necessarily know.

00:43:27.300 --> 00:43:30.915
So actually, before I
move on to the next slide,

00:43:30.915 --> 00:43:33.540
and this kind of thing happens
all the time, where you're like,

00:43:33.540 --> 00:43:35.340
come up with a great
thing small scale.

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:37.720
And it just doesn't maybe
pan out large scale.

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:39.220
So what's the difference?

00:43:39.220 --> 00:43:44.220
I'm going to spend a few slides
talking about Kaiyue and Tengyu

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:47.260
and Percy and David's
paper on basically doing

00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:51.180
a large-scale set of experiments
on comparing optimizers

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:53.505
and trying to do that
comparison better.

00:43:53.505 --> 00:43:55.880
And there's a lot to learn
about scaling and other things

00:43:55.880 --> 00:43:59.000
from that, but also just
standard deep learning stuff.

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:00.440
There's really basic things.

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:03.762
Like, the hyperparameters for
different models are different.

00:44:03.762 --> 00:44:05.720
So if you're scaling
things up, maybe they even

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:07.340
have different
scaling exponents.

00:44:07.340 --> 00:44:09.780
So you have to tune
Adam really well.

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:13.483
So if you tune it badly you
get this darker, brown line.

00:44:13.483 --> 00:44:15.400
And you're like, wow,
this thing is much worse

00:44:15.400 --> 00:44:17.608
than all these other algorithms
that I have over here

00:44:17.608 --> 00:44:18.275
on the left.

00:44:18.275 --> 00:44:20.400
But then you change the
learning rate a little bit.

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:23.640
And you're like, wow, actually,
all my gains went away.

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:26.880
Similarly on the right, you can
have cases where maybe you've

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:28.160
tuned your learning rate well.

00:44:28.160 --> 00:44:30.160
But now you've got other
stuff like weight decay

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:31.740
that is not optimally tuned.

00:44:31.740 --> 00:44:33.760
And so if you use the
same weight decay,

00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:36.040
then you're certainly going
to get a much worse result

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:37.040
for this green line.

00:44:37.040 --> 00:44:38.660
If you pick this
small weight decay,

00:44:38.660 --> 00:44:41.560
you'll get a much worse
result for this green line

00:44:41.560 --> 00:44:43.360
than if you pick the
optimal weight decay.

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:45.940
So there's all sorts of
pretty tricky things going on,

00:44:45.940 --> 00:44:48.413
technically not a scaling
thing, but a very important

00:44:48.413 --> 00:44:50.080
general empirical
machine learning thing

00:44:50.080 --> 00:44:53.730
that I want to point out.

00:44:53.730 --> 00:44:57.530
But maybe more relevant to the
discussion in this class right

00:44:57.530 --> 00:45:00.430
now is the scale
dependence of these things.

00:45:00.430 --> 00:45:04.090
And I think this is a good
case study and example of how

00:45:04.090 --> 00:45:06.370
to really think about
algorithm development

00:45:06.370 --> 00:45:09.250
as a function of scale,
because to me, there

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:11.970
are two variables that
are always important when

00:45:11.970 --> 00:45:12.990
thinking about scale.

00:45:12.990 --> 00:45:17.930
And this paper exposes, to
most extent, both of them.

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:20.390
So one axis is compute.

00:45:20.390 --> 00:45:24.730
And if you're fixing the
model size to data ratio,

00:45:24.730 --> 00:45:26.490
like the two are
coupled together,

00:45:26.490 --> 00:45:29.550
then this is essentially a
compute axis on the x-axis here.

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:35.010
So whenever you're doing a study
essentially scale up the compute

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:39.390
and look at the trends
as a function of scale.

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:40.970
And we see right
here that if we look

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:44.090
at the relative speedup
between Adam, which

00:45:44.090 --> 00:45:47.390
is at 1.0 by definition, and
all these other alternatives,

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:49.530
we see that muons are
really good at small scale.

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:53.530
And that gain diminishes
down as we increase in scale.

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:56.550
That's not really
necessarily the picture

00:45:56.550 --> 00:45:58.790
that you want to see.

00:45:58.790 --> 00:46:00.750
There's another axis
that you should always

00:46:00.750 --> 00:46:03.070
be vigilant of if you're
doing algorithm development,

00:46:03.070 --> 00:46:05.070
and the other one that
you should be vigilant of

00:46:05.070 --> 00:46:06.210
is the Chinchilla ratio.

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:08.550
So the Chinchilla ratio
can be thought of.

00:46:08.550 --> 00:46:10.950
It is the ratio
between how much data

00:46:10.950 --> 00:46:12.930
you have and the amount
of model you have.

00:46:12.930 --> 00:46:15.790
And this is a really, really,
really important parameter

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:17.670
because some
algorithms work well

00:46:17.670 --> 00:46:19.550
in the overparameterized
case where

00:46:19.550 --> 00:46:22.150
you have a way bigger model
than you have lots of data.

00:46:22.150 --> 00:46:25.550
Maybe your algorithm adds
some implicit regularization.

00:46:25.550 --> 00:46:28.990
Or maybe it is data
inefficient, but it works well

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:30.750
because of compute reasons.

00:46:30.750 --> 00:46:33.550
So some algorithms might work
well at the small Chinchilla

00:46:33.550 --> 00:46:35.030
ratio case.

00:46:35.030 --> 00:46:37.310
In contrast, other
algorithms might work well

00:46:37.310 --> 00:46:39.110
in the large Chinchilla
ratio case, where

00:46:39.110 --> 00:46:43.410
you have lots of data
per unit parameter

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:45.910
because you can pack
knowledge more efficiently

00:46:45.910 --> 00:46:46.890
into the parameters.

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:49.750
So this is a big,
big big confounder.

00:46:49.750 --> 00:46:52.890
Even papers that I think have
pretty good hygiene in doing

00:46:52.890 --> 00:46:57.355
scaling experiments on the
model size axis often neglect

00:46:57.355 --> 00:46:58.730
to or just don't
have the compute

00:46:58.730 --> 00:47:02.710
to study the Chinchilla
token to parameter ratio.

00:47:02.710 --> 00:47:05.427
And this turns out to be
surprisingly important

00:47:05.427 --> 00:47:06.010
a lot of time.

00:47:06.010 --> 00:47:07.070
In this case, it's not.

00:47:07.070 --> 00:47:11.490
In this case, you see
that the optimizer gains

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:12.713
are pretty consistent.

00:47:12.713 --> 00:47:14.130
The ratio is between
all the lines

00:47:14.130 --> 00:47:16.110
are the same at different
Chinchilla ratios.

00:47:16.110 --> 00:47:17.633
But this is not always the case.

00:47:17.633 --> 00:47:20.050
You always want to be vigilant
about both of these scaling

00:47:20.050 --> 00:47:20.590
factors.

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:23.290
That's quite important.

00:47:23.290 --> 00:47:25.850
And then I'm going
to also point out,

00:47:25.850 --> 00:47:29.770
if you have a new
algorithm or a new setting,

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:32.110
establishing scaling
is quite nontrivial.

00:47:32.110 --> 00:47:35.570
So I've taken this from one of--

00:47:35.570 --> 00:47:37.710
Will Held was
working with Marin,

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:41.930
which was Percy's open-source
language model training project.

00:47:41.930 --> 00:47:43.690
And there they were
basically trying

00:47:43.690 --> 00:47:46.630
to prove out a particular
set of hyperparameters.

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:48.120
This is even like
a new algorithm.

00:47:48.120 --> 00:47:50.060
So this is cautious
AdamC, which is

00:47:50.060 --> 00:47:52.820
like an Adam variant,
square root, batch size

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:55.880
scaling and a bunch of other
standard-looking things.

00:47:55.880 --> 00:47:59.540
And they're doing essentially
the same Chinchilla analysis

00:47:59.540 --> 00:48:02.300
that you've seen all
the other papers do.

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:04.200
But because this is not
necessarily a paper,

00:48:04.200 --> 00:48:06.520
this is a bunch of
people doing experiments,

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:08.460
you don't just publish
the successful one.

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:11.520
They have the niceness of
publishing their failed runs.

00:48:11.520 --> 00:48:13.020
And the example
here is that they're

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:15.380
doing basically standard
Chinchilla stuff,

00:48:15.380 --> 00:48:16.660
and it's looking great.

00:48:16.660 --> 00:48:19.020
If you go up to this
dashed vertical line at 10

00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:22.660
to the 20 something, this is a
really beautiful-looking scaling

00:48:22.660 --> 00:48:23.560
loft here.

00:48:23.560 --> 00:48:25.888
And then, as you scale
up, it's like, well, it's

00:48:25.888 --> 00:48:26.680
a little bit worse.

00:48:26.680 --> 00:48:27.720
It's a lot worse.

00:48:27.720 --> 00:48:30.600
And then, wow, now that
run is not so good anymore.

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:33.900
So it's totally blown up on you.

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:36.700
And the point here is that
even good-looking scaling

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:40.860
trends for many orders of
magnitude can suddenly bite you.

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:42.580
It can deviate from
linear, and then it

00:48:42.580 --> 00:48:44.760
can then blow up immediately.

00:48:44.760 --> 00:48:47.680
And in their case,
the fix here is just

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:51.400
picking more careful
muP-style parameterizations.

00:48:51.400 --> 00:48:52.460
Optimizer changes.

00:48:52.460 --> 00:48:54.680
They switch to a different
kind of optimizer,

00:48:54.680 --> 00:48:57.900
and they get nicer scaling
across more orders of magnitude.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:01.080
But the point here is to
say, when I go back here,

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:03.620
and I say, OK, well, you should
all do scaling experiments,

00:49:03.620 --> 00:49:05.287
you should scale all
your learning rates

00:49:05.287 --> 00:49:07.320
and so on, that's not easy.

00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:10.520
You end up fairly nontrivial
fraction of the time

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:12.498
with plots that look like this.

00:49:12.498 --> 00:49:14.040
And it's all horrible,
and you're not

00:49:14.040 --> 00:49:15.980
quite sure what has gone wrong.

00:49:18.460 --> 00:49:18.960
Good.

00:49:18.960 --> 00:49:21.360
OK.

00:49:21.360 --> 00:49:24.000
And then, so the last
thing I want to end with

00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:25.840
is just talking a
little bit about muon

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:29.960
and the closing
of the muon story.

00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:33.680
So I feel like I have to talk
about muon in a little bit

00:49:33.680 --> 00:49:36.920
more detail than I already
have, just because it's

00:49:36.920 --> 00:49:40.160
beginning to be integrated
into a large scale model

00:49:40.160 --> 00:49:41.080
training runs.

00:49:41.080 --> 00:49:44.900
That's the inclusion criteria
for a lot of new research

00:49:44.900 --> 00:49:46.340
topics for the class.

00:49:46.340 --> 00:49:48.300
Has it been in a
big training run?

00:49:48.300 --> 00:49:50.180
And muon now checks that mark.

00:49:50.180 --> 00:49:52.080
And it's also just an
interesting algorithm,

00:49:52.080 --> 00:49:54.460
so I'll mention it and talk
about it for a few minutes

00:49:54.460 --> 00:49:55.860
and then move on.

00:49:55.860 --> 00:49:58.300
So in gradient
descent, we usually

00:49:58.300 --> 00:50:01.100
have a fairly simple
thing that's happening.

00:50:01.100 --> 00:50:04.560
Ignore line for the moment,
sorry, line 5 for the moment.

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:06.060
And then you're
going to get roughly

00:50:06.060 --> 00:50:09.320
a standard
momentum-based optimizer.

00:50:09.320 --> 00:50:10.540
I take a gradient.

00:50:10.540 --> 00:50:12.400
I add it to B of t.

00:50:12.400 --> 00:50:16.100
This is my momentum step,
so I continue my momentum

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:17.760
from before with parameter mu.

00:50:17.760 --> 00:50:21.160
And then I update my parameters
based on this momentum gradient.

00:50:21.160 --> 00:50:24.420
Very standard momentum
gradient stuff.

00:50:24.420 --> 00:50:28.060
But then I think the really
interesting and, to me at least,

00:50:28.060 --> 00:50:30.920
a bit of an out of the box
set of ideas is to say,

00:50:30.920 --> 00:50:33.800
well, gradients have this
property that they treat,

00:50:33.800 --> 00:50:35.340
all the parameters are the same.

00:50:35.340 --> 00:50:38.900
But we know that not all the
parameters in a language model,

00:50:38.900 --> 00:50:40.920
or even a deep neural
network, are the same.

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:43.860
Some parameters are
like vector parameters.

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:45.680
They belong to the RMS norm.

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:47.580
Some parameters are
matrix parameters,

00:50:47.580 --> 00:50:49.900
like for the
attention or for MLPs.

00:50:49.900 --> 00:50:52.520
And matrix parameters
are probably

00:50:52.520 --> 00:50:54.740
different than
vector parameters.

00:50:54.740 --> 00:50:56.740
And so that's the starting
point for this idea

00:50:56.740 --> 00:50:58.760
and I think a really
interesting one.

00:50:58.760 --> 00:51:02.280
And so the one thing that you
could do about matrix parameters

00:51:02.280 --> 00:51:04.460
is you can start to
look at their spectra.

00:51:04.460 --> 00:51:05.700
They are matrices.

00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:08.920
Matrices have eigenvalues
and singular values.

00:51:08.920 --> 00:51:10.980
And so you can do things
to the singular values.

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:15.060
And so what you do here in Muon
is this fairly simple idea.

00:51:15.060 --> 00:51:16.420
You compute a gradient.

00:51:16.420 --> 00:51:18.440
You take a momentum step.

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:20.640
And now I take my update B of t.

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:22.120
And instead of
directly applying it

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:24.747
to my parameters in line
6, what I'm going to do

00:51:24.747 --> 00:51:26.580
is I'm going to do this
NewtonSchultz thing,

00:51:26.580 --> 00:51:28.960
NewtonSchultz 5 to be specific.

00:51:28.960 --> 00:51:33.567
And what that is it's going to
orthogonalize the B of t matrix.

00:51:33.567 --> 00:51:35.400
And so I've written the
operation down there

00:51:35.400 --> 00:51:36.320
on the bottom.

00:51:36.320 --> 00:51:37.520
Let's say I take B of t.

00:51:37.520 --> 00:51:42.130
I write it as singular value
decomposition, USV transpose.

00:51:42.130 --> 00:51:45.130
And then I make all of
my singular values 1.

00:51:45.130 --> 00:51:46.910
So if my singular
values are really big,

00:51:46.910 --> 00:51:48.350
I'll shrink them back to unit.

00:51:48.350 --> 00:51:50.058
If they're really
small, I'll expand them

00:51:50.058 --> 00:51:51.110
back up to being unit.

00:51:51.110 --> 00:51:54.130
And then I take UV transpose,
and that's my new update

00:51:54.130 --> 00:51:55.430
that I'm going to apply.

00:51:55.430 --> 00:51:59.250
If you want intuition
for this, and you already

00:51:59.250 --> 00:52:01.690
have intuition for something
like adagrad or these other,

00:52:01.690 --> 00:52:03.050
or Adam even.

00:52:03.050 --> 00:52:05.130
In adagrad or Adam,
you're going to divide

00:52:05.130 --> 00:52:07.210
by the size of the gradient.

00:52:07.210 --> 00:52:11.530
So every coordinate is roughly
going to be the same size.

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:14.910
In muon, you are operating
in the spectral norm instead.

00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:18.625
So every kind of direction
is going to be unit size.

00:52:18.625 --> 00:52:20.750
And notice that this only
makes sense for a matrix.

00:52:20.750 --> 00:52:24.810
I can only define this notion of
a singular value decomposition

00:52:24.810 --> 00:52:26.790
for a matrix valued B of t.

00:52:26.790 --> 00:52:28.890
So the interesting
thing about muon

00:52:28.890 --> 00:52:31.130
is it's a matrix
optimizer, so you apply it

00:52:31.130 --> 00:52:32.950
to these matrix
valued parameters.

00:52:32.950 --> 00:52:34.770
And for the
vector-valued ones, you

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:36.650
might still use
something like AdamW

00:52:36.650 --> 00:52:38.930
because those don't really
have any interaction

00:52:38.930 --> 00:52:40.990
with the NewtonSchultz term.

00:52:40.990 --> 00:52:43.550
Also, NewtonSchultz
is an approximation

00:52:43.550 --> 00:52:46.390
to that operation that only
uses matrix multiplies.

00:52:46.390 --> 00:52:48.510
So it's systems
efficient and not

00:52:48.510 --> 00:52:51.670
exactly the orthogonalization.

00:52:51.670 --> 00:52:55.210
But OK, why have I spent all
this time talking about muon?

00:52:55.210 --> 00:52:58.990
Well, first of all, it's
very powerful for things

00:52:58.990 --> 00:53:00.530
like the NanoGPT speedrun.

00:53:00.530 --> 00:53:03.590
It was discovered for
this set of things.

00:53:03.590 --> 00:53:05.910
And so you might think
that it works well.

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:08.030
As I said in the
previous slide, there

00:53:08.030 --> 00:53:09.790
have been some
scaling studies that

00:53:09.790 --> 00:53:15.430
have argued that the gain for
muon decreases as scale goes up.

00:53:15.430 --> 00:53:19.990
But then I want to close off
this story, which, I think,

00:53:19.990 --> 00:53:20.990
initially this came out.

00:53:20.990 --> 00:53:22.970
And I was like, wow,
muon is interesting.

00:53:22.970 --> 00:53:24.530
I wonder if anyone
will scale it up.

00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:26.830
And then [INAUDIBLE] and
others scaled up muon.

00:53:26.830 --> 00:53:29.350
And they said, well, maybe
it doesn't work super well.

00:53:29.350 --> 00:53:31.330
And I thought the
story was closed here.

00:53:31.330 --> 00:53:33.110
I was like, well,
maybe this is done.

00:53:33.110 --> 00:53:36.510
No one will spend the big
compute to scale this guy up

00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:38.170
to the full pre-training size.

00:53:38.170 --> 00:53:41.170
And then Kimi K2 comes
out, and that whole model

00:53:41.170 --> 00:53:43.930
is trained with muon,
fully with muon,

00:53:43.930 --> 00:53:46.050
with a few bells and
whistles to prevent muon

00:53:46.050 --> 00:53:47.270
from blowing up on you.

00:53:47.270 --> 00:53:50.050
They found a whole bunch
of instability issues.

00:53:50.050 --> 00:53:52.930
But Kimi K2 is an
outstandingly good model.

00:53:52.930 --> 00:53:57.930
It's a very good model, and the
training curves look reasonable.

00:53:57.930 --> 00:54:00.430
And so really, what
this suggests is like,

00:54:00.430 --> 00:54:02.230
well, muon worked at scale.

00:54:02.230 --> 00:54:03.630
Is it better than Adam?

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:05.550
Kimi K2 certainly doesn't
have any ablation,

00:54:05.550 --> 00:54:07.350
so we don't know at that scale.

00:54:07.350 --> 00:54:11.210
But it is certainly a valid
and workable solution.

00:54:11.210 --> 00:54:12.990
And so what's the
point of this story?

00:54:12.990 --> 00:54:15.250
It's to say, It's really
hard to whether something

00:54:15.250 --> 00:54:16.250
works at scale.

00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:18.410
It's very, very hard.

00:54:18.410 --> 00:54:21.130
But thus far, a lot of
small scale experiments

00:54:21.130 --> 00:54:23.090
and transferring them
up to large scale

00:54:23.090 --> 00:54:25.030
has been the way by
which we do science.

00:54:25.030 --> 00:54:26.770
And so you still want
to respect things

00:54:26.770 --> 00:54:28.430
like the NanoGPT speedrun.

00:54:28.430 --> 00:54:30.852
We learn quite a bit from
that and get good ideas.

00:54:30.852 --> 00:54:33.310
And then eventually, they make
their way to the big models.

00:54:33.310 --> 00:54:36.450
We'll see if people continue
to do ablations at big scale

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:39.700
and really nail down, is muon
better than AdamW at scale

00:54:39.700 --> 00:54:40.200
and so on?

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:42.460
Those are interesting
questions to which we don't yet

00:54:42.460 --> 00:54:44.620
have the answers.

00:54:44.620 --> 00:54:46.813
OK, I'll pause
here for a moment.

00:54:46.813 --> 00:54:48.480
This is the end of
my optimizer section.

00:54:48.480 --> 00:54:51.082
I want to make sure everyone's
good, also especially because I

00:54:51.082 --> 00:54:52.040
was talking about muon.

00:54:52.040 --> 00:54:54.420
And that's a whole
new can of worms

00:54:54.420 --> 00:54:56.900
that I haven't talked
about much before.

00:54:56.900 --> 00:54:58.020
Yeah?

00:54:58.020 --> 00:55:02.460
[INAUDIBLE] letter
[INAUDIBLE] faster on GPU?

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:03.260
OK, [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:03.760
Great.

00:55:03.760 --> 00:55:06.500
Yeah, the question was,
is SVD fast on GPUs?

00:55:06.500 --> 00:55:09.440
SVD is not fast on GPUs,
but they're not doing SVD.

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:11.080
What they're doing
is NewtonSchultz.

00:55:11.080 --> 00:55:14.420
NewtonSchultz is a matrix
multiply only finite iteration

00:55:14.420 --> 00:55:17.460
algorithm that approximates
the process of orthogonalizing

00:55:17.460 --> 00:55:18.940
a matrix.

00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:21.940
So there's several
clever pieces here.

00:55:21.940 --> 00:55:24.660
One of them is the
idea of treating it

00:55:24.660 --> 00:55:26.205
as a matrix on its own.

00:55:26.205 --> 00:55:28.080
And then there's this
orthogonalization idea,

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:29.580
and then there's
the systems piece

00:55:29.580 --> 00:55:30.920
of doing it with NewtonSchultz.

00:55:30.920 --> 00:55:33.840
So those are all important,
interesting pieces?

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:34.820
Yeah, back there?

00:55:34.820 --> 00:55:40.120
Are hyperparameters for
nontrivial [INAUDIBLE] ones?

00:55:40.120 --> 00:55:43.040
Are the hyperparameters here--
no, they're different, yeah.

00:55:43.040 --> 00:55:45.240
I think there are
several people that

00:55:45.240 --> 00:55:48.200
have been working on this
muon-style line of work.

00:55:48.200 --> 00:55:50.640
And one of them, I think,
like Jeremy Bernstein,

00:55:50.640 --> 00:55:52.520
I think he's been
saying, actually

00:55:52.520 --> 00:55:54.840
every layer should have
its own learning rate.

00:55:54.840 --> 00:55:56.600
I think the ultimate
limit of this

00:55:56.600 --> 00:55:59.520
is like, you go
from-- or even, sorry,

00:55:59.520 --> 00:56:01.960
every layer should
have its own optimizer.

00:56:01.960 --> 00:56:03.520
The ultimate limit
of this is to say,

00:56:03.520 --> 00:56:06.260
every parameter in a
transformer is different.

00:56:06.260 --> 00:56:08.660
Why not have a different
optimizer for each one of them?

00:56:08.660 --> 00:56:10.535
And so you would have
to tune hyperparameters

00:56:10.535 --> 00:56:13.660
for each one of them as well,
which I do not want to do.

00:56:13.660 --> 00:56:16.020
But that's maybe the
future of optimization.

00:56:16.020 --> 00:56:17.160
Yes?

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:20.000
What's the validity of like--

00:56:20.000 --> 00:56:22.980
like, on the learning
rate and batch size,

00:56:22.980 --> 00:56:25.240
you just consider
these two things.

00:56:25.240 --> 00:56:27.080
And then you pick the
best out of those,

00:56:27.080 --> 00:56:29.760
and then you can use it
to tune other things.

00:56:29.760 --> 00:56:34.380
But do these things
interact with each other?

00:56:34.380 --> 00:56:37.500
Yeah, or maybe the version of
the question that I interpreted

00:56:37.500 --> 00:56:39.620
was, a lot of these
people are just

00:56:39.620 --> 00:56:41.120
tuning learning
rate and batch size.

00:56:41.120 --> 00:56:42.700
And they do this on the grid.

00:56:42.700 --> 00:56:44.620
But presumably, these things
interact with a whole bunch

00:56:44.620 --> 00:56:45.640
of other hyperparameters.

00:56:45.640 --> 00:56:46.840
Aren't you worried about that?

00:56:46.840 --> 00:56:48.220
And I think, in
general, yes, you

00:56:48.220 --> 00:56:50.880
are worried about
hyperparameter interactions.

00:56:50.880 --> 00:56:53.540
But realistically, they
grow exponentially.

00:56:53.540 --> 00:56:56.940
This is not a thing where you
can grid out the whole space.

00:56:56.940 --> 00:56:59.980
And so I think the thing
that people often do

00:56:59.980 --> 00:57:03.100
is they grid the stuff that's
most concerning and most

00:57:03.100 --> 00:57:03.617
sensitive.

00:57:03.617 --> 00:57:05.200
And learning rates
are very important.

00:57:05.200 --> 00:57:07.158
I think, if you ever tune
stuff, learning rates

00:57:07.158 --> 00:57:08.880
are the most important
thing to tune.

00:57:08.880 --> 00:57:10.705
So they very
aggressively go on that.

00:57:10.705 --> 00:57:12.080
And then stuff
like weight decay,

00:57:12.080 --> 00:57:14.260
they might do a univariate
sweep afterwards

00:57:14.260 --> 00:57:19.160
to be locally optimal with
respect to those parameters.

00:57:19.160 --> 00:57:19.660
Good.

00:57:19.660 --> 00:57:22.700
OK.

00:57:22.700 --> 00:57:25.020
All right, so the final
part of this lecture

00:57:25.020 --> 00:57:27.060
is I want to talk about muP.

00:57:27.060 --> 00:57:29.440
muP is a bit of a
mysterious object.

00:57:29.440 --> 00:57:32.290
Even with many papers
written about it

00:57:32.290 --> 00:57:35.470
and, in fact, even
many implementations,

00:57:35.470 --> 00:57:36.990
I don't think all
of them even agree

00:57:36.990 --> 00:57:39.230
on what the underlying math
is or what the underlying

00:57:39.230 --> 00:57:41.030
implementation should be.

00:57:41.030 --> 00:57:43.650
But there's a core set
of ideas that are shared,

00:57:43.650 --> 00:57:45.690
and it does seem
generally effective.

00:57:45.690 --> 00:57:48.790
And so I'll talk about
my interpretation

00:57:48.790 --> 00:57:51.590
of the whole program,
let's call it.

00:57:51.590 --> 00:57:54.130
So the goal hopefully
is very clear.

00:57:54.130 --> 00:57:58.030
And I think this picture is the
goal, the picture being, well,

00:57:58.030 --> 00:57:59.750
if we increase the
size of our model,

00:57:59.750 --> 00:58:02.790
in this case, just width, our
optimal learning rate normally

00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:03.590
shifts.

00:58:03.590 --> 00:58:05.870
But ideally, we want the
optimal learning rate

00:58:05.870 --> 00:58:07.830
to be the same, no matter what.

00:58:07.830 --> 00:58:09.670
That is the game that
we would like to play.

00:58:09.670 --> 00:58:12.230
And the knobs that I'm willing
to tune to do this is I'm

00:58:12.230 --> 00:58:14.110
willing to mess with
the initializations

00:58:14.110 --> 00:58:15.568
at a per-layer basis.

00:58:15.568 --> 00:58:17.110
I'm willing to mess
with the learning

00:58:17.110 --> 00:58:20.575
rates on a per-parameter
basis as well.

00:58:20.575 --> 00:58:22.950
And then I'm sometimes willing
to scale up or down things

00:58:22.950 --> 00:58:27.110
like residual connections
based on the size of my model.

00:58:27.110 --> 00:58:29.510
So that's the game
that I'd like to play.

00:58:29.510 --> 00:58:31.310
And how well does
it work in practice?

00:58:31.310 --> 00:58:34.450
There's been several papers
that have studied these fairly

00:58:34.450 --> 00:58:36.650
extensively.

00:58:36.650 --> 00:58:39.890
Cerebras which is really
a chip company, also

00:58:39.890 --> 00:58:42.490
has a language
model training arm

00:58:42.490 --> 00:58:46.410
run by Hestnes of the
scaling law paper fame.

00:58:46.410 --> 00:58:49.850
And I feel like they do very
interesting things because they

00:58:49.850 --> 00:58:53.850
now are writing lots of papers
about muP scaling of language

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:58.010
models, and they have Cerebras
GPT from a couple of years ago,

00:58:58.010 --> 00:59:02.970
where they were training the 0.1
to 13b models using the usual

00:59:02.970 --> 00:59:07.550
Chinchilla recipe, but also
including a muP variant,

00:59:07.550 --> 00:59:10.410
which they use to try to
tune the hyperparameters more

00:59:10.410 --> 00:59:12.610
effectively.

00:59:12.610 --> 00:59:14.930
And I think one of the
nice things about this

00:59:14.930 --> 00:59:19.250
is they find that if they
use the muP parameterization,

00:59:19.250 --> 00:59:22.310
then their scaling law fits
are generally more stable.

00:59:22.310 --> 00:59:26.370
Their projected muP trends
are almost right on the money

00:59:26.370 --> 00:59:27.950
for their actual models.

00:59:27.950 --> 00:59:30.390
Whereas, I think
their non-muP models

00:59:30.390 --> 00:59:34.550
fluctuated much more wildly in
terms of the loss predictions

00:59:34.550 --> 00:59:35.165
that they had.

00:59:35.165 --> 00:59:36.290
And so their claim was, OK.

00:59:36.290 --> 00:59:38.550
MuP is maybe a really good idea.

00:59:38.550 --> 00:59:40.250
So this is validated at scale.

00:59:40.250 --> 00:59:42.150
You saw MiniCPM on others.

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:46.190
Many people have trained
models with muP variants.

00:59:46.190 --> 00:59:49.870
So I could also talk a lot
about training or examples

00:59:49.870 --> 00:59:51.530
of models trained with muP.

00:59:51.530 --> 00:59:53.990
But I think maybe the more
interesting thing for me

00:59:53.990 --> 00:59:55.830
to do right now is
actually to talk

00:59:55.830 --> 01:00:01.735
about the conceptual foundations
and the math for muP.

01:00:01.735 --> 01:00:03.110
And if you're
interested in this,

01:00:03.110 --> 01:00:05.490
and you want to read a
somewhat accessible paper,

01:00:05.490 --> 01:00:06.810
I would recommend this one.

01:00:06.810 --> 01:00:11.110
This is the paper that I think,
for me, was the clearest.

01:00:11.110 --> 01:00:12.210
There are several papers.

01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:14.650
Greg Yang, who started
this research program,

01:00:14.650 --> 01:00:17.730
has a series of papers
I'll call tensor programs.

01:00:17.730 --> 01:00:20.110
I find them somewhat
inscrutable.

01:00:20.110 --> 01:00:23.250
Jeremy Bernstein, who was also
working on the muon stuff,

01:00:23.250 --> 01:00:27.680
has written this nice-- not
recap paper, like a review

01:00:27.680 --> 01:00:29.840
paper, putting it into a
different framework, which

01:00:29.840 --> 01:00:31.250
I think is very accessible.

01:00:31.250 --> 01:00:33.000
And there are several
others by physicists

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:36.180
that are also interesting in
terms of explaining what muP is.

01:00:36.180 --> 01:00:39.520
The Cerebras people actually
do a bit of that as well.

01:00:39.520 --> 01:00:44.040
But anyway, returning to this
paper, the core idea of muP

01:00:44.040 --> 01:00:46.600
is based off of two
kind of assertions.

01:00:46.600 --> 01:00:49.280
And this is a very physicist
way of thinking about this.

01:00:49.280 --> 01:00:51.360
So if I make my
network big-- let's

01:00:51.360 --> 01:00:53.280
say I'm only changing
the width for now.

01:00:53.280 --> 01:00:55.840
I'm making the width
of my network big.

01:00:55.840 --> 01:00:58.160
I'm going to assert two
invariants that should

01:00:58.160 --> 01:00:59.940
hold as I do the scaling limit.

01:00:59.940 --> 01:01:03.580
First of all, the activations
at initialization--

01:01:03.580 --> 01:01:06.300
so if I initialize my model,
and I put in some random data,

01:01:06.300 --> 01:01:09.720
the activations should
remain the same size roughly

01:01:09.720 --> 01:01:11.120
as a function of the network.

01:01:11.120 --> 01:01:13.500
So if they blow up
with the network size,

01:01:13.500 --> 01:01:15.620
or they shrink to 0
with the network size,

01:01:15.620 --> 01:01:17.540
I have chosen a wrong
parameterization.

01:01:17.540 --> 01:01:18.800
And that makes sense.

01:01:18.800 --> 01:01:21.920
My activation should be roughly
the same scale, regardless

01:01:21.920 --> 01:01:23.960
of the size of my network.

01:01:23.960 --> 01:01:28.220
Now, after one gradient step,
the change in my activation

01:01:28.220 --> 01:01:29.580
should be O of 1.

01:01:29.580 --> 01:01:31.560
So this is what people
call feature learning.

01:01:31.560 --> 01:01:34.580
So feature learning means if
I take a gradient step, things

01:01:34.580 --> 01:01:37.080
in my network change
a significant amount

01:01:37.080 --> 01:01:39.580
and that this amount
should be a fixed function

01:01:39.580 --> 01:01:41.420
of the width of the network.

01:01:41.420 --> 01:01:44.260
In case some of
you are theorists,

01:01:44.260 --> 01:01:46.920
you might be familiar with
neural tangent kernels.

01:01:46.920 --> 01:01:49.420
Neural tangent kernels would
have a very different behavior.

01:01:49.420 --> 01:01:50.837
Those would be the
kinds of things

01:01:50.837 --> 01:01:52.660
where the change in
activations actually

01:01:52.660 --> 01:01:54.767
vanish as a function
of the network width.

01:01:54.767 --> 01:01:57.100
And you don't want that,
because you do want the network

01:01:57.100 --> 01:01:59.620
to change and learn
features and do things even

01:01:59.620 --> 01:02:02.580
in the limit of your
big neural networks.

01:02:02.580 --> 01:02:04.160
So these two are the invariants.

01:02:04.160 --> 01:02:05.000
They make sense.

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:07.940
They're basically saying,
activations and the change

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:10.580
in the activations should
remain roughly similar

01:02:10.580 --> 01:02:12.460
as a function of the
width of the network.

01:02:12.460 --> 01:02:15.008
And then note that
I'm going to sometimes

01:02:15.008 --> 01:02:17.300
go back and forth between
individual activations, which

01:02:17.300 --> 01:02:17.960
is this.

01:02:17.960 --> 01:02:19.820
And then the norm, which--

01:02:19.820 --> 01:02:21.800
if each activation
is roughly O of 1,

01:02:21.800 --> 01:02:24.580
the norm of the whole
vector of activation

01:02:24.580 --> 01:02:26.800
should be square
root of the number

01:02:26.800 --> 01:02:29.120
of hidden units in that layer.

01:02:29.120 --> 01:02:31.760
OK, so that's the main thing.

01:02:31.760 --> 01:02:34.240
Now, the thing about
muP is it's interesting

01:02:34.240 --> 01:02:38.640
because it starts with this
very simple and palatable set

01:02:38.640 --> 01:02:39.343
of assumptions.

01:02:39.343 --> 01:02:41.760
You might all agree that this
is a reasonable thing to do.

01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:44.160
And then we're going
to make a number

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:46.340
of additional
strong assumptions.

01:02:46.340 --> 01:02:47.760
But then, in the
end, we're going

01:02:47.760 --> 01:02:49.360
to get a very simple
set of scaling

01:02:49.360 --> 01:02:51.800
rules that drop out of this.

01:02:51.800 --> 01:02:53.888
So remember, what
we want to do is

01:02:53.888 --> 01:02:55.680
we want to make sure
that the activation is

01:02:55.680 --> 01:02:57.340
at init are roughly unit.

01:02:57.340 --> 01:02:58.480
So what do we do?

01:02:58.480 --> 01:03:01.120
Well, if we have a simple
deep linear network,

01:03:01.120 --> 01:03:02.880
and we initialize
all of our weights

01:03:02.880 --> 01:03:07.040
as a sigma squared
scaled Gaussian,

01:03:07.040 --> 01:03:10.060
then what is going to be the
spectral norm of this matrix?

01:03:10.060 --> 01:03:11.320
Well, you can write that down.

01:03:11.320 --> 01:03:12.987
I'm not going to ask
you to verify that,

01:03:12.987 --> 01:03:14.880
but that's standard
matrix concentration

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:18.720
arguments for the operator norm
of a random Gaussian matrix.

01:03:18.720 --> 01:03:21.880
And then now note that
in certain regimes where

01:03:21.880 --> 01:03:25.570
the fan-out is, I think, not
much bigger than the fan-in,

01:03:25.570 --> 01:03:28.210
you're going to get that
the output of the layer

01:03:28.210 --> 01:03:31.810
is similar to the product
of the input of the layer

01:03:31.810 --> 01:03:33.370
times the operator norm.

01:03:33.370 --> 01:03:35.010
This is always an
upper bound, but this

01:03:35.010 --> 01:03:39.130
is approximate in certain
high-dimensional regimes.

01:03:39.130 --> 01:03:41.830
Now, if this is true,
now what can we do?

01:03:41.830 --> 01:03:43.850
Well, we can solve
for sigma to try

01:03:43.850 --> 01:03:47.090
to keep our activations the
same across all the layers

01:03:47.090 --> 01:03:48.610
and across all the width.

01:03:48.610 --> 01:03:51.730
So imagine that at
layer L minus 1,

01:03:51.730 --> 01:03:55.390
I have square root of
L minus 1 activation.

01:03:55.390 --> 01:03:58.650
And this is, remember,
consistent with this notation

01:03:58.650 --> 01:04:00.530
that, at least for
this whole vector,

01:04:00.530 --> 01:04:02.110
I should have square
root of n of L.

01:04:02.110 --> 01:04:04.890
So this is our base
case for induction.

01:04:04.890 --> 01:04:07.530
And then our inductive
case says, well,

01:04:07.530 --> 01:04:09.690
remember that I have this
operator norm at layer

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:12.690
L, which is this function of
the fan in and the fan out.

01:04:12.690 --> 01:04:15.770
And so this is going
to be equal to this

01:04:15.770 --> 01:04:17.910
if I pick a particular sigma.

01:04:17.910 --> 01:04:19.757
So let's say this is the ansatz.

01:04:19.757 --> 01:04:21.590
I'm going to assume
that this is the answer,

01:04:21.590 --> 01:04:23.632
and then we'll see what
happens to the induction.

01:04:23.632 --> 01:04:27.150
If I pick this as a hypothesis,
and I plug this into the sigma,

01:04:27.150 --> 01:04:29.830
notice that I get that the
ratio of the operator norm

01:04:29.830 --> 01:04:31.402
is this, the ratio
of the fan out

01:04:31.402 --> 01:04:34.110
over the-- sorry, square root of
the fan-out over the square root

01:04:34.110 --> 01:04:35.110
of the fan-in.

01:04:35.110 --> 01:04:37.110
And then I plug
this back in here.

01:04:37.110 --> 01:04:39.750
And then I'm going to get
that the norm of the output

01:04:39.750 --> 01:04:42.310
is going to be equal to the
square root of the number

01:04:42.310 --> 01:04:44.950
of units in my layer plus
some lower-order terms

01:04:44.950 --> 01:04:46.890
that I'm not going to
bother to keep track of.

01:04:46.890 --> 01:04:49.430
So where this
sigma came out of--

01:04:49.430 --> 01:04:51.650
I mean, this is drawing
it out of a hat.

01:04:51.650 --> 01:04:56.670
But we can verify that if I
plug this noise initialization

01:04:56.670 --> 01:05:00.470
into my formula, then,
essentially, at every layer,

01:05:00.470 --> 01:05:03.390
the size of my
activations is roughly

01:05:03.390 --> 01:05:05.470
equal to the square root
of the number of hidden

01:05:05.470 --> 01:05:07.568
units at that layer.

01:05:07.568 --> 01:05:09.110
There are some
assumptions made here,

01:05:09.110 --> 01:05:10.650
like this approximate equals.

01:05:10.650 --> 01:05:15.270
But this is mostly a
legitimate set of assumptions.

01:05:15.270 --> 01:05:18.550
So condition A1
is pretty simple.

01:05:18.550 --> 01:05:23.410
Condition A2 is a little
bit hairier, to be honest.

01:05:23.410 --> 01:05:26.410
So really, we need to
deal with the updates now.

01:05:26.410 --> 01:05:30.250
And I'm only going to consider
the case of a single example

01:05:30.250 --> 01:05:34.450
in a batch for SGD for
linear neural networks, so

01:05:34.450 --> 01:05:36.212
deep linear network.

01:05:36.212 --> 01:05:37.670
So what is this
going to look like?

01:05:37.670 --> 01:05:42.490
Well, the change in my weights
for a particular layer L--

01:05:42.490 --> 01:05:44.670
if you think about how
this is going to behave,

01:05:44.670 --> 01:05:48.290
it's going to be a rank 1 update
between my gradient for the loss

01:05:48.290 --> 01:05:49.910
and the activations.

01:05:49.910 --> 01:05:53.030
This is true just because of
the behavior of the backwards.

01:05:53.030 --> 01:05:55.750
The forwards is a
rank 1 multiplication.

01:05:55.750 --> 01:05:59.850
The backwards is going to
be a rank 1 outer product.

01:05:59.850 --> 01:06:02.710
So now this is the
change in the weights.

01:06:02.710 --> 01:06:06.570
And now what is the change in
the activations at that layer?

01:06:06.570 --> 01:06:08.930
Well, the change in
the activations--

01:06:08.930 --> 01:06:13.710
I just take h equals W times hl
minus 1, and I expand this out.

01:06:13.710 --> 01:06:15.590
And I expand this out
into this formula.

01:06:15.590 --> 01:06:17.330
This is going to be
exactly the change

01:06:17.330 --> 01:06:18.918
in the activation at layer L.

01:06:18.918 --> 01:06:20.460
And it's going to
have two terms, one

01:06:20.460 --> 01:06:23.500
that is the direct term from
changes in the activation at L

01:06:23.500 --> 01:06:27.320
minus 1 and the cross term
in some sense of what happens

01:06:27.320 --> 01:06:28.820
because my weights
have changed, not

01:06:28.820 --> 01:06:31.487
just because the activations of
the previous layer have changed.

01:06:31.487 --> 01:06:34.520
So there's two terms
to this update.

01:06:34.520 --> 01:06:38.460
And so this is the change in
the activation at layer L.

01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:40.900
And now what I'm
going to do is I'm

01:06:40.900 --> 01:06:42.993
going to look through
each of these three terms.

01:06:42.993 --> 01:06:44.660
I want each of these
three terms to have

01:06:44.660 --> 01:06:47.740
the same order of magnitude, and
I want that order of magnitude

01:06:47.740 --> 01:06:50.360
to be square root of
n of L. Why is that?

01:06:50.360 --> 01:06:52.760
Remember that, if I
go back two slides,

01:06:52.760 --> 01:06:56.000
the condition that I want for A2
is that after one gradient step,

01:06:56.000 --> 01:06:59.360
the change in activation
should be O of 1 or omega of 1.

01:06:59.360 --> 01:07:01.260
And so if I want
that to be true,

01:07:01.260 --> 01:07:04.860
then the norm of that layer
should be square root of nl.

01:07:04.860 --> 01:07:08.140
And so to do that, what I want
is for each of these three terms

01:07:08.140 --> 01:07:10.120
to have that order of magnitude.

01:07:10.120 --> 01:07:12.980
And if that's true, and
there's no cancelations,

01:07:12.980 --> 01:07:15.680
then this is going to be the
right order of magnitude.

01:07:15.680 --> 01:07:17.960
You can see the sketchy
pieces adding up.

01:07:17.960 --> 01:07:20.520
We're assuming things like
no cancelations and so on,

01:07:20.520 --> 01:07:22.760
but this is giving us
some interesting ways

01:07:22.760 --> 01:07:25.740
of thinking about the magnitude
of each of these objects.

01:07:25.740 --> 01:07:28.720
So at this point, the right
way to think about this

01:07:28.720 --> 01:07:30.760
is not like, OK,
Tatsu is sitting here

01:07:30.760 --> 01:07:32.620
doing rigorous mathematics.

01:07:32.620 --> 01:07:34.520
What I am doing here
is I'm basically

01:07:34.520 --> 01:07:38.040
keeping track of all the orders
of magnitude of all the terms.

01:07:38.040 --> 01:07:40.540
And I am saying that,
given our assumptions,

01:07:40.540 --> 01:07:42.060
how big should these roughly be?

01:07:42.060 --> 01:07:43.640
It's physicists math.

01:07:43.640 --> 01:07:45.440
No offense to physicists.

01:07:45.440 --> 01:07:49.760
This was developed by
physicists in this way.

01:07:49.760 --> 01:07:52.560
OK, so now really-- sorry,
go back one slide just

01:07:52.560 --> 01:07:54.600
to make all this clear.

01:07:54.600 --> 01:07:57.600
So the first term, this
comes by almost assumption.

01:07:57.600 --> 01:07:59.340
It's the inductive assumption.

01:07:59.340 --> 01:08:01.600
We assume that
delta h of l minus 1

01:08:01.600 --> 01:08:03.180
has the right
order of magnitude.

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:05.160
So this comes for free.

01:08:05.160 --> 01:08:07.540
These other two terms--
these are trickier.

01:08:07.540 --> 01:08:09.840
We have these
terms that multiply

01:08:09.840 --> 01:08:12.840
the change in my weights
in operator norm times

01:08:12.840 --> 01:08:14.580
square root of L minus 1.

01:08:14.580 --> 01:08:17.140
And so because these two
are the order of magnitude

01:08:17.140 --> 01:08:20.020
of both of these
terms, we know how big

01:08:20.020 --> 01:08:21.245
this delta L needs to be.

01:08:21.245 --> 01:08:22.787
We can solve for
this because we know

01:08:22.787 --> 01:08:26.279
that this left-hand side
needs to be square root and L.

01:08:26.279 --> 01:08:29.000
And then we have this term, so
we solve for this operator norm.

01:08:29.000 --> 01:08:30.640
And that's exactly
this quantity.

01:08:30.640 --> 01:08:33.500
So this is going to be the
goal for the next slide.

01:08:33.500 --> 01:08:36.620
I want to try to figure out
what kinds of learning rates

01:08:36.620 --> 01:08:39.740
are going to give me an
update that's roughly scaled

01:08:39.740 --> 01:08:42.340
as the square root of the
fan-out over the square root

01:08:42.340 --> 01:08:43.380
of the fan-in.

01:08:43.380 --> 01:08:44.540
So that's the goal.

01:08:44.540 --> 01:08:47.279
As I said, after I
pick a learning rate,

01:08:47.279 --> 01:08:49.240
this should roughly be true.

01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:50.859
So how can we do that?

01:08:50.859 --> 01:08:53.279
Well, we're going to make
yet another assumption.

01:08:53.279 --> 01:08:56.080
And this is probably the least
palatable of the assumptions,

01:08:56.080 --> 01:08:57.620
in my opinion,
which is that we're

01:08:57.620 --> 01:09:00.220
going to assume that
the loss update is

01:09:00.220 --> 01:09:01.640
going to be scaled by unit.

01:09:01.640 --> 01:09:04.020
In other words,
we're going to assume

01:09:04.020 --> 01:09:05.740
that our model is
actually making

01:09:05.740 --> 01:09:09.180
some appreciable, nontrivial
progress as a function of scale

01:09:09.180 --> 01:09:11.620
and that that's roughly similar.

01:09:11.620 --> 01:09:13.282
Not quite sure that's
palatable to me,

01:09:13.282 --> 01:09:14.740
but that is yet
another assumption.

01:09:14.740 --> 01:09:15.800
So we throw that down.

01:09:15.800 --> 01:09:17.560
But now the point
of this assumption

01:09:17.560 --> 01:09:19.920
is that if this is true,
the rest of the math

01:09:19.920 --> 01:09:22.840
works out quite cleanly,
because the change in the loss

01:09:22.840 --> 01:09:25.040
is the product of
my gradient plus

01:09:25.040 --> 01:09:26.380
how much my weights changed.

01:09:26.380 --> 01:09:27.960
This is Taylor approximation.

01:09:27.960 --> 01:09:29.840
And then because
this is rank 1, I

01:09:29.840 --> 01:09:31.800
can rewrite this thing
as a Frobenius norm

01:09:31.800 --> 01:09:33.920
and as an operator norm.

01:09:33.920 --> 01:09:37.620
And so this right-hand side now
allows us to get what we want.

01:09:37.620 --> 01:09:39.080
This is O of 1.

01:09:39.080 --> 01:09:42.080
This term we want to be square
root n over square root l

01:09:42.080 --> 01:09:42.700
minus 1.

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:44.160
That's this term
on the right here.

01:09:44.160 --> 01:09:47.560
And then finally, I have
this gradient of L term.

01:09:47.560 --> 01:09:49.640
And this is going to
be square root of NL

01:09:49.640 --> 01:09:51.560
minus 1 over square root nl.

01:09:51.560 --> 01:09:54.180
And then so now
what eta do I need?

01:09:54.180 --> 01:09:56.240
Well, the eta that
I need is going

01:09:56.240 --> 01:09:57.960
to be solving for
this quantity, which

01:09:57.960 --> 01:10:02.280
is the ratio of these two guys,
which is nl over nl minus 1.

01:10:02.280 --> 01:10:05.560
So we made this one assumption
that the left-hand side of this

01:10:05.560 --> 01:10:06.265
is O of 1.

01:10:06.265 --> 01:10:08.140
And then we've solved
for all the quantities,

01:10:08.140 --> 01:10:11.040
and we've ended up with a
fan-out over fan-in ratio

01:10:11.040 --> 01:10:13.770
for a learning rate for a layer.

01:10:13.770 --> 01:10:16.310
One side note that I'll
make is that for Adam,

01:10:16.310 --> 01:10:20.450
because of the changes in some
of this, you're going to get a 1

01:10:20.450 --> 01:10:24.170
over nl minus 1 scaling,
which I'll mention here.

01:10:24.170 --> 01:10:29.410
So I've walked through a bunch
of hopefully intuitive-looking

01:10:29.410 --> 01:10:32.057
scaling arguments for
various components.

01:10:32.057 --> 01:10:33.890
But in the end, maybe
you are interested in,

01:10:33.890 --> 01:10:34.950
what does this mean?

01:10:34.950 --> 01:10:37.250
What does this do for me?

01:10:37.250 --> 01:10:38.770
What this means is
that we're going

01:10:38.770 --> 01:10:42.090
to change how we set
our initializations

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:44.210
and learning rates a little bit.

01:10:44.210 --> 01:10:48.290
So our initialization is going
to look like-- so for muP,

01:10:48.290 --> 01:10:49.795
the blue box is what we do.

01:10:49.795 --> 01:10:51.170
Our initialization
is going to be

01:10:51.170 --> 01:10:55.010
1 over square root nl minus
1 times a component that

01:10:55.010 --> 01:10:57.670
is 1 over the square
root fanout over fanin.

01:10:57.670 --> 01:11:00.810
So if this is 1, then
this is equivalent

01:11:00.810 --> 01:11:02.270
to the usual initialization.

01:11:02.270 --> 01:11:04.290
This is the standard
parameterization.

01:11:04.290 --> 01:11:06.998
But if this ratio is
not smaller than 1,

01:11:06.998 --> 01:11:08.790
then you get basically
something different.

01:11:08.790 --> 01:11:10.873
You get something that's
closer to the square root

01:11:10.873 --> 01:11:13.030
fanout over fanin.

01:11:13.030 --> 01:11:14.790
The learning rate
normally you would

01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:18.022
set to be a constant as a
function of your model size,

01:11:18.022 --> 01:11:20.230
but you would want to set
this to the ratio of fanout

01:11:20.230 --> 01:11:21.070
and fanin.

01:11:21.070 --> 01:11:23.478
And maybe more interestingly,
for Adam, this whole thing

01:11:23.478 --> 01:11:24.770
is a little bit more different.

01:11:24.770 --> 01:11:27.390
I'm not going to even touch
the derivation for that, where

01:11:27.390 --> 01:11:30.150
you drop this 1 over
nl term, and you get 1

01:11:30.150 --> 01:11:34.270
over the fanin as the layer
specific learning rate.

01:11:34.270 --> 01:11:37.510
And so basically, layers
that have big fanins for Adam

01:11:37.510 --> 01:11:38.990
get smaller learning rates.

01:11:38.990 --> 01:11:41.750
It allows you to have these
layer-adaptive learning rates

01:11:41.750 --> 01:11:44.030
that are quite interesting.

01:11:44.030 --> 01:11:49.237
So that was the
derivation of muP.

01:11:49.237 --> 01:11:50.570
I went through a lot of details.

01:11:50.570 --> 01:11:52.390
I do want to maybe
talk about what

01:11:52.390 --> 01:11:54.210
the high-level
point of that was.

01:11:54.210 --> 01:11:56.470
And the high-level
point of that is, muP

01:11:56.470 --> 01:11:58.690
as an argument is
very interesting.

01:11:58.690 --> 01:12:01.710
Not only is there
actual useful stuff.

01:12:01.710 --> 01:12:03.550
The way in which you
get to the algorithm

01:12:03.550 --> 01:12:06.110
is itself interesting because
it's this very different kind

01:12:06.110 --> 01:12:08.150
of math in which
you're saying, OK,

01:12:08.150 --> 01:12:10.590
I'm going to take a scaling
limit of my network.

01:12:10.590 --> 01:12:13.027
And then I assert some
invariance about my network,

01:12:13.027 --> 01:12:15.110
and then I'm going to make
additional assumptions.

01:12:15.110 --> 01:12:16.735
And then I'm going
to try to figure out

01:12:16.735 --> 01:12:18.743
what the constraints on
my hyperparameters are.

01:12:18.743 --> 01:12:20.410
And the constraints
essentially give you

01:12:20.410 --> 01:12:24.050
certain kinds of scaling limits
on your hyperparameters as well.

01:12:24.050 --> 01:12:26.770
So this is a general
principle for coming up

01:12:26.770 --> 01:12:29.250
with different kinds of
algorithms or hyperparameter

01:12:29.250 --> 01:12:30.850
scalings, I think,
which are probably

01:12:30.850 --> 01:12:35.330
very different than what you're
used to in CS or ML training.

01:12:35.330 --> 01:12:38.650
OK, those are the
muP derivation.

01:12:38.650 --> 01:12:41.010
I want to close out on
the muP part with just

01:12:41.010 --> 01:12:43.130
a few empirical things.

01:12:43.130 --> 01:12:47.410
There's an interesting paper
by an independent researcher

01:12:47.410 --> 01:12:52.290
on basically stress testing
muP in lots of different ways.

01:12:52.290 --> 01:12:56.410
And muP can be thought
of as a procedure

01:12:56.410 --> 01:12:58.970
for tuning hyperparameters.

01:12:58.970 --> 01:13:01.583
And so depending on
how you do things,

01:13:01.583 --> 01:13:03.250
Adam is going to have
a certain learning

01:13:03.250 --> 01:13:05.710
rate as a function of different
components of the network.

01:13:05.710 --> 01:13:08.840
So you have an embedding
parameter, attention parameters,

01:13:08.840 --> 01:13:10.703
input/output MLPs
or softmax linears.

01:13:10.703 --> 01:13:12.620
All of those are going
to have different kinds

01:13:12.620 --> 01:13:18.540
of scalings under muP, as
well as not muP scaling rules.

01:13:18.540 --> 01:13:23.740
And the interesting thing
here is that particular paper

01:13:23.740 --> 01:13:25.700
replicated a bunch
of the muP results

01:13:25.700 --> 01:13:28.880
for language models trained
at different scales.

01:13:28.880 --> 01:13:32.740
And maybe unsurprisingly, he
found almost the headline result

01:13:32.740 --> 01:13:35.860
from MiniCPM and
others, which is

01:13:35.860 --> 01:13:38.480
that if you get the muP right,
and you're only scaling with,

01:13:38.480 --> 01:13:40.740
in this case, in a
very controlled way,

01:13:40.740 --> 01:13:44.300
you get exactly the
learning rate optimality

01:13:44.300 --> 01:13:47.180
invariance that baseline muP--

01:13:47.180 --> 01:13:49.780
or just looking at a
variant of muP that

01:13:49.780 --> 01:13:53.500
tracks projection biases
for attention, both of those

01:13:53.500 --> 01:13:55.380
transfer very nicely.

01:13:55.380 --> 01:13:59.820
But then you might
ask, basically,

01:13:59.820 --> 01:14:01.780
there's a lot of things
in the real world

01:14:01.780 --> 01:14:04.220
that muP does not handle.

01:14:04.220 --> 01:14:07.680
Technically, SwiGLU
does not fit into muP,

01:14:07.680 --> 01:14:11.320
neither does initialize
variations, initialization

01:14:11.320 --> 01:14:12.600
or RMS norm.

01:14:12.600 --> 01:14:14.420
Or if you really mess
with your optimizer,

01:14:14.420 --> 01:14:15.800
that will mess that up, too.

01:14:15.800 --> 01:14:17.175
And so you might
ask, like, which

01:14:17.175 --> 01:14:19.680
of these things that are
not allowed by the theory

01:14:19.680 --> 01:14:20.700
really break muP?

01:14:20.700 --> 01:14:24.480
And actually, for the most part,
these mostly work with muP.

01:14:24.480 --> 01:14:26.400
But some of the
things that don't work

01:14:26.400 --> 01:14:30.840
is if you learn the
gain term in RMS norm,

01:14:30.840 --> 01:14:32.760
this turns out to break muP.

01:14:32.760 --> 01:14:34.960
Although, these can be
removed in many cases

01:14:34.960 --> 01:14:38.000
without necessarily
hurting you, and so maybe

01:14:38.000 --> 01:14:39.380
it's not such a bad idea.

01:14:39.380 --> 01:14:41.680
But this does seem to break muP.

01:14:41.680 --> 01:14:44.200
It also seems to break if you
use more exotic optimizers

01:14:44.200 --> 01:14:47.740
like Lion, which basically rely
on the sign of the gradient.

01:14:47.740 --> 01:14:51.000
And sign gradient is
like a spiritually

01:14:51.000 --> 01:14:52.523
similar to muon and other ideas.

01:14:52.523 --> 01:14:54.440
And so there might be
other interesting things

01:14:54.440 --> 01:14:58.080
that break with muP as well.

01:14:58.080 --> 01:15:00.960
Finally, the thing that
seems most concerning

01:15:00.960 --> 01:15:04.360
is that if you do large
decoupled weight decay,

01:15:04.360 --> 01:15:06.920
you end up with
significant muP failure.

01:15:06.920 --> 01:15:11.140
So this is maybe the one
stress test that it does fail.

01:15:11.140 --> 01:15:14.060
But to close out this final
section, you might, in the end,

01:15:14.060 --> 01:15:16.300
ask, like, is muP useful?

01:15:16.300 --> 01:15:19.280
And if you really want to
stabilize your learning rates,

01:15:19.280 --> 01:15:21.420
it is actually pretty useful.

01:15:21.420 --> 01:15:23.620
There's a lot of experiments
of this form, where

01:15:23.620 --> 01:15:26.340
if you increase the width, your
optimal learning rate shifts

01:15:26.340 --> 01:15:30.000
very predictably, but very
greatly as a function of scale.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:31.320
Whereas, with muP it doesn't.

01:15:31.320 --> 01:15:34.020
And so it's one of the
many tools in the toolkit

01:15:34.020 --> 01:15:37.797
to try to control hyperparameter
drift as a function of scale.

01:15:37.797 --> 01:15:39.880
I think it's an interesting
open area of research.

01:15:39.880 --> 01:15:42.540
It's not really done
and dusted, and this

01:15:42.540 --> 01:15:44.040
is the one right thing to do.

01:15:44.040 --> 01:15:46.860
But there's a lot of promise
in the muP-style initialization

01:15:46.860 --> 01:15:47.443
program.

01:15:47.443 --> 01:15:49.860
There's also a lot of promise
in just fitting scaling laws

01:15:49.860 --> 01:15:52.140
as well.

01:15:52.140 --> 01:15:56.740
So final thing I want to close
with, scaling in the wild

01:15:56.740 --> 01:15:57.520
is very tricky.

01:15:57.520 --> 01:16:00.260
As I mentioned in
one of my replies.

01:16:00.260 --> 01:16:02.780
I think the initial
presentation of scaling laws

01:16:02.780 --> 01:16:04.550
makes it sound like a science.

01:16:04.550 --> 01:16:07.110
It's like, yes, draw this
line, do these procedures,

01:16:07.110 --> 01:16:10.590
and you will know, for sure,
what will happen at scale.

01:16:10.590 --> 01:16:14.270
But I think, in reality, it's
a lot more messy and a lot more

01:16:14.270 --> 01:16:16.150
unknown than that.

01:16:16.150 --> 01:16:17.910
There are lots of
things that people

01:16:17.910 --> 01:16:19.750
do use in practice
scaling laws for,

01:16:19.750 --> 01:16:22.150
like picking architectures,
picking optimizers, picking

01:16:22.150 --> 01:16:23.010
hyperparameters.

01:16:23.010 --> 01:16:24.910
All of these things
are things that people

01:16:24.910 --> 01:16:27.895
do with scaling laws, but
there is really an art to it

01:16:27.895 --> 01:16:29.270
in the sense of,
you don't really

01:16:29.270 --> 01:16:31.650
if it's truly going to
extrapolate forever.

01:16:31.650 --> 01:16:34.590
You want to do reasonable
things to maximize

01:16:34.590 --> 01:16:35.770
the chances of success.

01:16:35.770 --> 01:16:38.390
And so there's lots of
solutions that people have.

01:16:38.390 --> 01:16:39.450
You can use muP.

01:16:39.450 --> 01:16:41.910
You can search for the
optimal learning rate in all

01:16:41.910 --> 01:16:43.370
of these other kinds of things.

01:16:43.370 --> 01:16:46.150
And these are ways of
controlling hyperparameter

01:16:46.150 --> 01:16:46.650
drift.

01:16:46.650 --> 01:16:48.090
But there's no
silver bullet yet.

01:16:48.090 --> 01:16:49.990
Maybe next year,
there will be a module

01:16:49.990 --> 01:16:53.390
that's like, we solved
it, but not quite yet.

01:16:53.390 --> 01:16:55.270
Great Thanks a lot.

01:16:55.270 --> 01:16:58.220
I can take questions afterwards.

